class_name Lore
extends RefCounted
## Every foe has a story (the Fates and Faces release): who or what it was before, what happened to
## it, and why it stands in the road now.
##
## BEAST: the ordinary monsters of every region.  The first battle against a species shows its story
## on a card over the battle's opening (battle.gd), and every species met is kept in the Bestiary
## (Esc > Bestiary).
## BOSS: the bosses, the Gate Generals and the Heavenly Tribulation.  Before a boss's battle the
## narrator tells its story (Main.boss_story, once a boss), and the Bestiary keeps it.
## (Part XIV's foes and the fated encounters' foes are at the end of each.)

const BEAST := {
	# ---- Part XXII: The High Roads
	"salt_thief": "The small kin of the shanxiao: knee-high, one-footed, with heels that point the wrong way, and unable to pass a bag of salt. They come to a camp after the fire has burned down, take the salt and whatever lies nearest it, and are gone up the rock on one leg faster than a man on two. A woodcutter who wakes and laughs is followed home. One who wakes and says nothing finds his wood stacked.",
	"false_echo": "Call across a valley and the valley answers. In the Mulberry Silk Woods this spring it answers first. A false echo is a voice that has come loose from whoever owned it and goes about the slopes looking for the rest of its sentence. It does no harm by itself. The harm is in turning round to see who spoke.",
	"fire_crab": "The old books say the shanxiao comes down to travellers' fires at night to roast the crabs it has caught in the streams, and steals their salt to season them. These are the crabs: brook crabs as broad as a hat, carried to so many fires that they have begun to go to them of their own accord, and wait in the embers.",
	"stray_gust": "When the Earl of the Wind drew his bag shut, some of what was outside it stayed outside: eddies, cat's-paws, the draught under a door. With no weather to belong to they have drawn together in the low places of Thunder Valley and gone round and round until they are something. They are not angry. They are only shut out.",
	"kite_hawk": "A hawk that hunts kites. It took the first paper swallow for a rival, the second for an insult and the third for prey, and has since grown a taste for split bamboo. It stoops on anything that hangs in the air without flapping, which this spring includes a good many people.",
	"wind_bag": "A wind-bag of the Earl's that was not tied tight: a hide sack the size of a calf that creeps along the ground by what leaks out of it, wheezing, and is drawn to anything warm. Step on one and it gives up a week of north wind at once.",
	"red_leopard": "She rides a red leopard, and striped lynxes follow, says the song. The red leopards of the Stone Forest do not hunt this spring. They lie across the paths with their heads on their paws, facing downhill, and get up only for somebody who is not the one expected. That has been everybody.",
	"fig_creeper": "The Mountain Spirit is clothed in fig leaves and girdled with hanging moss, and where she has stood a long time the dress takes root. The creepers of her cloud go hand over hand up anything that keeps still, a rock, a cairn, a scholar, and hold on with great tenderness, as to a sleeve.",
	"rain_gibbon": "Thunder rumbles, rain darkens; the gibbons mourn, howling all the night. They are the last line of her song, and they know it. When one of the grey gibbons of the karst lifts its face and calls, the cloud comes down another yard, and anybody on the tower loses sight of his own feet.",
	"merit_stele": "A dressed stone, taller than a man, cut with the name of somebody and what he wishes to be thought to have done. They stood at the foot of the mountain for a thousand years, because the top was the kings'. The night the cloud thinned they began to walk up. They are very slow, and nothing whatever will turn one aside.",
	"report_smoke": "When a king announced his success to Heaven he burned the announcement, that it might rise. Most of it rose. The smoke of the reports that were not quite true was too heavy, and has hung in the gullies of the peak ever since, still reciting: all under heaven is at peace, the harvests are good, the people rejoice.",
	"bends_serpent": "The path up the last of the peak turns eighteen times, and every turn has had a bearer's curse laid on it by each man who ever carried a sedan chair round it. The serpents of the bends are those turns, got up off the mountain. Each is exactly as long as the stretch between two corners, and as steep.",
	# Part XIX (the Hundred Works release)
	"wheel_mite": "Leave a wheel turning with nobody at it and something will come to live in it, as mice come to a barn. A wheel-mite is a knot of bronze legs round one toothed wheel, and it has a single idea, which is that the wheel should turn. It oils. It tightens. It is the most diligent creature in the land, and it has never once asked what the wheel is for.",
	"treadle_imp": "Every loom and lathe has a treadle, and a foot that works one for ten hours goes on twitching in its sleep. The twitch, in a summer when the machines want for nothing, got up by itself. It is brown, bandy, and always in step, and it will pump any pedal it finds until the thing flies apart.",
	"idle_shuttle": "A shuttle is made to go back and forth, and the worst thing in the world for it is to lie in a basket. When the looms of Qingshi began to weave by water, the hand-shuttles were put away, and at night they come out of the baskets and fly the lanes, to and fro, to and fro, weaving nothing, with a bit of red thread trailing behind.",
	"cog_crab": "A gear that has slipped its shaft is only a wheel with teeth, and it will lie where it fell for a thousand years. Unless there are a great many of them, and a pit that does not sleep. Then they find each other, mesh, and walk: sideways, like any crab, because a train of gears has no notion of forwards.",
	"bellows_wraith": "The water-bellows breathes all night, and a breath that goes in and out with no lungs round it is halfway to a ghost already. The founders say you can see it over the stacks at the turn of the watch: a long grey shape that swells and sighs, swells and sighs, with a red glow where a man would have a mouth.",
	"hammer_golem": "A trip-hammer wants one thing, and it is not to be lifted: it is to fall. Nine days of falling with nothing under it but an anvil, and the hammers of Tushan pulled their own posts out of the ground and went looking for something that would make a better noise.",
	"brass_spiderling": "When the Brass Spider Engine fell in Heaven's Forge it lay where it fell, and the Forgemaster swept round it. This summer it came apart, very neatly, at every joint, into things the size of dogs: eight legs, a body like a lamp, and a counting-wheel for a face. They are not the Engine. They are the part of it that never stopped counting.",
	"counting_wasp": "The Engine was built to count mortals for the Jade Ledger, and it kept its tallies on little toothed wheels of gilt bronze. With nobody to read them the tallies took wing. A counting wasp hovers in front of a thing until it has the number of it, clicks once, and goes on to the next thing. It has never been wrong and nobody has ever asked.",
	"tally_golem": "Somebody has to carry the total. The spiderlings bring it their numbers and it adds them, slag and rivets and roof-tiles, on beads of bronze strung through its chest, and when it runs out of beads it makes more out of whatever is nearest. It is very large by now. It does not know what the sum is of.",
	"wooden_kite": "Gongshu Ban carved a magpie of wood and bamboo that stayed up three days, and Mozi told him it was a toy. It was not a toy to the magpie. Its children fly still: plank wings, a peg for an eye, and since this summer a purpose, which is to carry anything that ticks out into the west.",
	"stray_ox": "The Chancellor's wooden oxen carried a year's grain over the mountains and wanted no fodder, and when the war was over nobody thought to tell them. They are patient, and square, and they have been waiting at the same milestones for a very long time. This summer something in the dunes called them, and they went, with their panniers still on.",
	"paper_servant": "Cut from one sheet, folded once, breathed on: a paper servant stands a hand high and does what it is shown. These were shown something, by somebody, once, and never shown how to stop. They have grown to the height of a man by pasting themselves with every scrap of paper in the land, and their bows are still perfect.",
	# Part XXI (the Hundred Arms release)
	"sword_reed": "The reeds of the south country have always had an edge: a child who pulls one carelessly learns it. This winter they have grown straight, stiff and a yard long, with a ridge down the middle, and stand in the water in ranks. Nobody planted them so. The marsh has been listening to what the land wants, and the land wants blades.",
	"white_gibbon": "The pupils of the old ape, forty of them, who have been taught one stroke for two thousand years and practise it on reeds. They are not fierce. They are diligent, which in the matter of swords comes to much the same thing. Each copies whatever weapon last came into the marsh, with a reed, perfectly.",
	"hook_heron": "The men of Wu forged a hooked sword and were so proud of it that a smith killed his two sons to temper a pair with their blood. The heron of the southern rivers has had the same curve to its bill for longer than there have been smiths, and uses it for frogs. Lately it has been using it for other things.",
	"stray_arrow": "An arrow loosed at nothing, to see how far it would go, has never been told it has landed. On the cold steppe this winter there are five hundred of them, still flying in short flat rushes a hand above the snow, looking for whatever it was they were shot at. The horse-folk say each one must be finished by somebody who means it.",
	"wheel_louse": "Ji Chang hung a louse in his window on a hair of a yak's tail and looked at it for three years, until it was as big as a cartwheel. He never asked what became of the louse. A thing that has been looked at that hard, for that long, by the best eye under heaven, does not go back to being small.",
	"cup_snake": "A guest saw a snake in his wine cup, drank from politeness, and was ill for a month, until his host sat him in the same seat and showed him the bow on the wall and its reflection in the wine. The snake that is the shadow of a bow is real enough to those who have swallowed it, and on the steppe this winter every tent has a bow up.",
	"gate_swordsman": "Three thousand ate at the king's gate, with tousled hair and bristling beards, short coats, glaring eyes and rough speech, and fought in front of him day and night. Each came from somewhere: a caravan guard, a ferryman's brother, a boy with his grandfather's sword. None of them will say where, at the gate. It is against the custom of the place.",
	"fighting_cock": "Ji Xingzi trained a fighting cock for the king. After ten days: not yet, he is vain and quick to anger. After twenty: not yet, he starts at shadows. After forty it stood like a cock of wood, and no other bird would face it. These are the birds of the first ten days. The king's men keep them for luck, and they resemble their keepers.",
	"broken_edge": "When a sword breaks in a fight at the gate, the pieces are swept up with the sand and the tallies. But an edge does not stop being an edge for want of the rest of the sword. They drift about the yards of the red palace a hand above the ground, bright and short, each still going the way it was going when it broke.",
	"pool_blade": "King Helu of Wu was buried under a hill with three thousand swords, the Fish-Belly among them, and the First Emperor dug for them and found only water. The pool is still there. So were the swords, each in its hilt on the floor of it, quite content, until this winter, when every one of them heard that blades were walking.",
	"mound_tiger": "Three days after the king was laid in the hill, a white tiger came and crouched on the mound, and the place has been called Tiger Hill since. It is the breath of all that buried metal, the old books say, taking the only shape that fits it. It does not hunt. It sits on what it was set to guard, and gets up when the guard is broken.",
	"humming_sword": "In the Graveyard of Sword Saints every blade was laid down with a name on a tablet above it, and hummed a little when a bearer of the Seal went by, as a dog thumps its tail. These have had their tablets knocked down in the dark by newcomers that have no names at all, and a sword that is not sure which one it is hums on one note without stopping.",
	# Part XX (the Hollow Hills release)
	"cave_cricket": "A cricket of the deep caves has no eyes, no colour, and feelers three times the length of its body, with which it reads the dark as a blind man reads a face. They have lived in the same chambers since before the hills had names, and they do not come up. When they come up, something has taken their room.",
	"blind_newt": "The olm, the country people call it, or the dragon's baby: pink as a new mouse, a hand long, with frilled gills and no eyes at all. It can go ten years without eating and live a hundred. Everything about it is made for a place where nothing ever changes, and this autumn its place changed.",
	"drip_wisp": "One drop every nine heartbeats, falling in the dark for four hundred years, is a kind of counting, and a thing that is counted long enough begins to think it is somebody. A drip-wisp is the patience of a stalactite, got loose. It is cold, it keeps perfect time, and it resents being hurried.",
	"stone_bat": "The word for bat sounds like the word for good fortune, and so there are five of them carved over every door in the land. These are the other kind: grey as the roof they hang from, heavy as a roof-tile, and woken at noon by somebody tipping a cartload of spoil into their bedroom.",
	"dig_mole": "Where people dig, moles take an interest, as a carpenter takes an interest in another man's joinery. These have followed the pits of the land down and down, improving them, and have become large on it. They do not understand timbering and regard a pit-prop as a piece of poor work left in the way.",
	"ore_gnawer": "A rat's teeth never stop growing, and it must gnaw to keep them short. In the old workings of Tushan there is nothing to gnaw but ore. Forty generations on, there is a thing with a grey iron hide and teeth like chisels, which eats a seam the way a silkworm eats a leaf, and leaves the same neat edge.",
	"lamp_ghost": "A miner hangs his lamp where he works and takes it when he goes. A lamp left burning means he meant to come back. When he does not, the flame waits, and goes on waiting after the oil is gone: a small light a little way down a gallery, at the height of a man's hand, that is never any nearer.",
	"kept_voice": "Shout your name into a cave and it comes back. What is not asked is where it has been. In the Cave of Ten Thousand Echoes they did not all come back, and the ones that stayed had centuries to grow the rest of a person round a single word. Each one will tell you its word, if asked, and nothing else.",
	"hollow_one": "The dust of the Hollow of the Mountain, got up and walking in the shape of whoever last stood there: a founder with a lamp, two boys with a weiqi board. There is nothing inside them. That is not a misfortune to them. It is what they are made of, and they lie down again quite willingly.",
	"burrow_marmot": "The marmots of the pasture have always dug, and the horse-folk, who dig nothing, forgave them for it, a burrow being a very small hole. This autumn the ground is falling in under the burrows, and the marmots have come up angry, standing on their hind legs on every mound, whistling for somebody to blame.",
	"sink_toad": "Where a sinkhole opens and there is water at the bottom, there is a toad in the water by the next rain. Nobody has ever seen one arrive. They are the size of a washtub, the colour of wet stone, and they sing in the evening like a man clearing his throat at the bottom of a well.",
	"karst_crawler": "The limestone towers of the karst are hollow as old teeth, and something pale and many-legged has always lived in them, in the dark, eating what the bats leave. It has feelers where its eyes should be and is extremely shy. It is out in the daylight now, on the outside of the towers, and nobody is more upset about it than the crawler.",
	"bamboo_wolf": "The bamboo wolves once took only what the old forest spirit allowed them: a deer in a hard winter, a hare in spring. When the red moon soured the roots, the spirit stopped speaking to them, and the pack has hunted anything on the paths ever since.",
	"bandit": "Most of Iron Fang's men were porters and charcoal-burners from the villages below the mountain, ruined by the Court's road-tax and one bad harvest. They took up crossbows for one season, meaning to go home; the Blood Moon's money saw to it that none did.",
	"leaf_sprite": "Leaf sprites are the whispers of the Whispering Bamboo Forest: the old spirit's thoughts, blown loose on the wind and given small green bodies. Since the red moon got into the roots the thoughts have soured, and the sprites sting where they used to sing.",
	"mire_toad": "Mire toads grew fat on blood-lotus seed until their skins sweated venom, and the Marsh Hag milked them for the poison she sold to bandits by the gourd. They spit it at anything warm that wades too close, which in the Crimson Marsh is not often.",
	"bog_wraith": "The Court's maps squared the Crimson Marsh into fields, and settlers were sent to farm the squares; the red water took them before the first seed was in. Bog wraiths still wade the mire with their measuring cords, pacing out fields that were never there.",
	"poison_vine": "The poison vines began as the Marsh Hag's hedge, planted round her blood lotuses to keep thieves out and watered with whatever the thieves left behind. They outgrew the hedge long ago and creep across the marsh, feeling in the mud for warm ankles.",
	"frost_wolf": "Frost wolves ran before the Ice Empress's winter, herding the snow down the mountains each autumn and back up each spring. When her winter stopped ending they had nothing left to herd but travellers, and they still hunt in a ring, as if driving a storm.",
	"ice_golem": "No living courtier would stay in the Ice Empress's cold, so she carved a court out of the cavern ice: chamberlains, guards, a whole silent hall. They keep her etiquette still, and shut out any uninvited warmth as servants shut a door against a draught.",
	"snow_wraith": "Frost jades grow in the dark of the caverns like fruit, and men have always come to dig them. Some sat down to rest where the cold began to feel warm, and never stood up; snow wraiths are those diggers, drifting through the ice, still reaching for jade.",
	"storm_hawk": "Storm hawks nest in the lee of the Thunder Roc's crag and hunt in the storms it gathers, riding the downdraughts like leaves on a millrace. Since the Roc made the storm its throne, they have taken it as royal law that anything below the clouds is prey.",
	"thunder_ape": "The apes of Thunder Valley were the only creatures but the sword ghost who never flinched at thunder, so the ghost taught them a little. Too late he saw that they only wanted to hit harder; now they beat their chests and call lightning down on travellers.",
	"spark_wisp": "When storms lie down to rest between the crags of Thunder Valley, the sparks they shed wander off like children while their parents sleep. Spark wisps play with anything that rings when struck; a sword rings beautifully, and so does the hand that holds it.",
	"blood_acolyte": "Blood acolytes were the failures of a dozen sects, disciples told their qi would never stir. Xu Mo promised the moon's blood would carry them past every wall; they paid him a bowl of their own each night, called it a fair price, and some believe it still.",
	"bone_soldier": "Xu Mo's aura alone broke three sects, and their disciples did not stay in their graves. Bone soldiers still wear the colours of the schools that trained them, faded to one grey, and guard the palace that killed them because nobody has told them to stop.",
	"shadow_fiend": "The Blood Moon Palace drank the blood of those it took and left their shadows lying on its floors. General Mo Ying gathered the shadows into a flock and taught them to hunt; they cannot recall whose they were, and they cling to the living to borrow a shape.",
	"coral_guard": "Coral guards are crabs of the Dragon King's household who grew armour from the palace walls, a ring for every year of service. When the rift seized the King's heart its rot ran down his orders into them; they still bow towards the throne before they strike.",
	"kelp_serpent": "Kelp serpents once combed the Dragon Palace's sea-gardens and kept its pearl-beds clean, as mild as eels. The rift's rot came up through the holdfasts of the kelp, and now they wind round anything that swims within reach and squeeze until it stops swimming.",
	"abyss_jelly": "Abyss jellies were the living lamps of the Dragon King's feasts, drifting through the coral halls. When the rift opened beneath the palace they sank towards its cold light and rose again full of it, and their sting carries the numbness of the deep.",
	"magma_slime": "The Ashen Volcano sleeps over a wound in the world, and magma slimes are what bleeds from it: rock too hot to remember being rock. They crawl uphill towards anything with qi in it, as moths are drawn to a lamp, and leave trails of black glass behind them.",
	"ash_salamander": "Ash salamanders lived in the Phoenix's nest and fed on the ash of her rebirths, the softest and warmest food in the world. When she went mad the nest burned too hot even for them; driven down the slopes, they are hungry, homeless and furious with everyone.",
	"ember_sprite": "Every ember the Ash Phoenix shed in her madness that did not cool woke as a sprite, with a scrap of her pain in it. Ember sprites dance at travellers the way she once danced at the sky, and they burn as she burned: without meaning to, and without stopping.",
	"void_walker": "Wu Ji, the hermit of the Expanse, calls the void horrors the Emperor's tears. Void walkers are tears that fell through the thin places and learned to stand; there is no up or down here, so they walk forward for ever, through whatever is in their way.",
	"rift_spider": "Rift spiders are the Rift Weaver's brood, hatched in the dark between the drifting islands. They stitch the floating stones together with silk spun from the space between stars, and anything caught in that silk begins, slowly, to come apart at the edges.",
	"star_eater": "Where the land wore thin and the stars show through the ground, star eaters graze on the light like drifting jellyfish. The hermit warns travellers not to stare at the stars too long: star eaters are what stares back, and living qi is sweeter than starlight.",
	"celestial_guard": "The Celestial Guards were soldiers raised to heaven for their loyalty and promised the wages of immortals. The Court never paid them, not once in ten thousand years, and they still stand their watch at the Gate, waiting for their pay and an order to go home.",
	"thunder_qilin": "Thunder qilin were foaled by the Azure Qilin when the Gate was still kind, and grew up in the Emperor's storms. They have never known a quiet sky; they charge anything that does not smell of thunder, and their hooves leave scorched prints on the clouds.",
	"light_wisp": "The dawns the Jade Emperor could not stomach, when he swallowed the heavens, broke and fled to the Gate, the one place he never looked. Dawn wisps are those scraps of morning; they have forgotten light was meant to warm, and they blind whoever looks at them.",
	"mirror_wisp": "Every face that bent over the Mirror Lake left a little of its light in the water. Mirror wisps are those lights, each searching for the face it came from; they flare in a traveller's eyes, hoping to be recognised, and burn when they are not.",
	"glass_sentinel": "Glass sentinels were raised beside the Glass Colossus from lake-glass and caught lightning, to keep the Emperor's hands off the Mirror of Bearers. Time has crazed their glass, and they strike at every warm thing, forgetting whose hands they watched for.",
	"echo_shade": "The Mirror Lake's echoes mostly lie still; these curdled and grew teeth. An echo shade moves a heartbeat before you do, with your own stance and your own blade, as an echo would if it hated the voice it came from. A few still try to warn; most only bite.",
	"grave_hound": "Some sword saints were buried with the dogs that had guarded their doors. When the Emperor's grave-robbers came for the relics, the dogs rose to drive them off, and they have not lain down since: to a grave hound, every living footstep is a robber's.",
	"bone_knight": "Not every sword saint kept the oath. The few who sold the Seal's secrets were buried at the graveyard's edge, faces to the wall, and the Emperor's whistle raised them as his knights. They march the barrows still, guarding nothing, ashamed without knowing why.",
	"ghost_lantern": "Mourners lit a lantern on every new barrow in the graveyard, and some never went out. Each holds a saint's last wish in its flame and drifts between the graves at night, looking for whoever it was meant to light home, and burning whoever it finds instead.",
	"hourglass_wraith": "Whenever a tribulation swallowed an ascender's Dao, the hour they stood in was left behind on the river's banks with its sand still running. Hourglass wraiths are those hours. They are lonely, and they try to fill themselves with yours.",
	"clockwork_guard": "Tian Xuan set the clockwork guards along the river the day he took the throne of heaven, and wound them to march for ten thousand years, so nobody would reach the source and meet the boy he had been. The hand that wound them is gone; the guards still tick.",
	"dust_moth": "Dust moths hatch in forgotten days the way clothes-moths hatch in a chest of old robes, and they eat whatever nobody has remembered for years. A traveller from the present burns like a lamp on that dim river, and they come to it as moths do, to put it out.",
	"maw_tendril": "The Hollow Heaven woke hungry when the Emperor fell, and put out roots through the white stone of the Origin, feeling for anything that held a Dao. Each tendril ends in a small mouth, and each mouth remembers the taste of some ascender it was fed long ago.",
	"hollow_acolyte": "They came to the Origin of the Dao in search of the beginning of all things, and found the Hollow instead. It emptied them gently, as it empties everything, and left the robes standing; the acolytes still bow towards it, and still try to bring it offerings.",
	"dao_fragment": "A Dao fragment is what remains of an ascender's understanding after the harvest has chewed it: a sliver of insight into the world, crackling with the lightning that tore it loose. It drifts about the Origin, stinging like a thought one cannot finish.",
	"cloud_ray": "Feng Yu's rays once carried weary ascenders between the islands. The Court's clerks fixed tally-hooks to their fins and taught them to skim a year from every rider; they circle the islands still, collecting for masters who are gone.",
	"gale_sprite": "Born where the west wind first strikes the islands, gale sprites used to blow the clouds into stepping-stones for travellers. The Herald taught them to scatter the stones instead, stranding the unwary between islands until they paid their toll in years.",
	"sky_mantis": "The Gale Mantis Empress's children reap the high winds with scythes of green chitin and bind the gusts into sheaves, for the Court taxed even the wind. Nobody comes for the sheaves now, but they reap on, and a traveller looks very like wheat.",
	"ledger_clerk": "Failed scholars of a hundred dynasties, promised a seat in heaven, the clerks copied names for the Court until they forgot their own. No one ever told them to stop, so they stamp a debt on every step a stranger takes through the ruins.",
	"jade_lion": "Carved in pairs to guard the doors of the Court's pavilions, the jade lions were taught to smell an unpaid debt the way a dog smells a thief. Their doors have fallen, but they still pace the rubble, and every living traveller reeks of years owed.",
	"ink_wraith": "Pages spoiled by a blot or a scratched-out name were tipped into the archive's ink-pit, and over the ages the pit grew wraiths. Each is a ruined name that has forgotten whose it was, and it clings to the living, hoping to be written again.",
	"bronze_sentinel": "Cast in the forge's first year to keep thieves from the anvil, the bronze sentinels were given a single order: no unfinished work leaves the forge. To them, a cultivator not yet struck by the forge's lightning is exactly that.",
	"slag_slime": "Bolts that came out of the moulds too weak or too wild were skimmed off and tipped into the slag fields. The dross never cooled; it crawled together into slimes, each one a tribulation that was never sent and still aches to strike someone.",
	"bellows_imp": "Sparks that leapt from the great bellows whenever the Phoenix breathed out, the imps were made to fan the anvil-coals and nothing else. Since the orders stopped they fan whatever will burn, and nothing burns brighter than a cultivator's qi.",
	"peach_sprite": "A sprite wakes in each Garden blossom and lives until its peach ripens, three thousand years later. The Court counted every blossom into the Ledger as fruit owed and punished the sprites for each missing peach, so they guard their branches from every hand.",
	"garden_crane": "The Matriarch's flock carried the souls of fallen ascenders up to the Garden, gently, as cranes bear the dead in the old paintings. They learned too late where the Court was taking them, and now they stab at anyone who comes to take more.",
	"moss_tortoise": "The Court scratched its tallies of fruit and souls into the tortoises' shells, as the ancients wrote on oracle bones, and moss has long since grown over the columns. Each carries a page of the Ledger on its back and bites whoever tries to read it.",
	"dune_scorpion": "Shazhou children once kept dune scorpions in clay jars, for they were shy and ate only beetles. When the stars began to fall the scorpions burrowed into the craters and drank the light, and now their stings glow white and they fear nothing that walks.",
	"sand_wraith": "Every caravan the desert ever swallowed lies somewhere beneath the dunes, and when the falling stars shook the sand, its thirst rose without it. Sand wraiths drift towards the Sweet Well at dusk and fall on anyone who stands between them and water.",
	"meteor_golem": "When a star falls alone, far from its fellows, its pieces drag themselves together in the shape of the first thing they see, usually a nomad. Meteorite golems wander the dunes still hot from the sky, crushing anything between them and the western pillar.",
	"spore_puff": "The Mother's youngest, spore puffs are mushroom folk no taller than a child, who used to dance in the warm glow of Zhu Que's embers. Since her fire went cold they are frightened of everything, and a frightened puff bites, then bursts into sleep-dust.",
	"glow_moth": "For a hundred years the glow moths have circled the cold fire at the southern pillar with their Queen, trying to warm their wings. They carry a little of that fire in their bellies, a light that burns cold, and dash it against whatever comes near.",
	"root_crawler": "The southern pillar's roots once held its stone the way a hand holds a cup. As the rot set in, knots of root broke free and scuttled off on spindly legs, and now they gnaw at anything with qi in it, feeding the sickness they came from.",
	"frost_yak": "Generation after generation, the frost yaks walked one great circle of pasture under the lights, and the shamans walked with them. When the sky sank low enough to brush their horns the herds broke and stampeded south, trampling whatever will not run.",
	"aurora_wisp": "Each wisp is a breath the sky let out too fast, a flake of the northern lights that fell to the snow before it could rise again. They startle at a footstep, and a startled wisp lashes out with the lightning the aurora is made of.",
	"ice_lynx": "Lynx kits on the steppe learn one lesson from the Matriarch before they can walk: whoever comes north smelling of qi has come to take something. The Court's cultivators came for Xuan Wu's strength for an age, and the lynxes learned to hunt them down.",
	"chaos_eel": "Chaos eels swam in the colour-sea before there was a sky to eat. Now that the sky has worn thin they gather at its roots like eels round a drowned pier, gnawing at the seams Nuwa mended and at any living thing that swims too close.",
	"primal_jelly": "A primal jelly is a scrap of the world that was never finished: having no shape, it takes the shape of whatever it touches, and does not know why it wants to. Nuwa's stones kept them sleeping; as the stones drifted apart, the jellies woke.",
	"faceless_spawn": "Hundun dreams as he wakes, and each dream crawls out of the chaos on six legs, with four stubby wings and no face. The spawn do not know what they are; they press against anyone who has a face, hungry for one of their own.",
	"maple_fox": "Fox spirits have always haunted the funeral road, begging scraps from the mourners' offerings. Since the Wheel stopped, the Nine-Tailed Fox has taught them that a stray soul lantern tastes of a whole life, and now they snap at every light that drifts past.",
	"lost_lantern": "Paper lanterns light the dead down the gorge, one to every soul. Since the Wheel stopped, the lanterns have been drifting back up without their owners, lost and frightened, and a frightened lantern burns whatever comes too close.",
	"mossback_boar": "Mossback boars have rooted between the graves of the gorge for so long that grave-moss grows along their spines. They lived on beech-mast and maple seed until the dead began walking back up the road; now the ground is restless beneath them, and so are they.",
	"river_carp": "The carp of the Nine Bends lived on whatever the river carried: crumbs from funeral boats, the odd copper coin. Now the water is thick with memories the dead have dropped, and the carp gulp them until they cannot tell their own lives from a stranger's.",
	"stone_macaque": "The macaques of the Stone Forest, grey as the limestone, have always robbed travellers of rice cakes and sandals. Now they snatch memory pearls from the shallows and hoard them in the towers, and fight like demons to keep some stranger's grandmother's laugh.",
	"mist_heron": "Mist herons stand so still in the river fog that boatmen take them for reeds. They used to spear fish; now they spear the memories rising through the water, and each one swallowed sets them dreaming a life they never had. Wake one, and it strikes.",
	"hopping_corpse": "Bodies of travellers who died far from home, the hopping corpses were led back to their villages by Daoist corpse-drivers, hopping in line behind a bell. Since the Wheel stopped the Patriarch has drilled them into ranks, and they fall on anyone out of step.",
	"hungry_ghost": "Hungry ghosts are the dead nobody remembered to feed: no offerings, no paper money, no name read aloud at the Ghost Festival. They crowd the Naihe Bridge with throats too thin to swallow and bellies too big to fill, snatching at any warmth the living bring.",
	"lily_moth": "Spider-lily moths sip only from the red flowers of the Yellow Springs, and their wings carry the lilies' old rule: the living and the dead never meet. They swarm anyone who walks the meadows with a heartbeat, beating burning wings to drive them home.",
	"ghost_clerk": "The Clerks of the Dead were souls who died owing the Court more years than they had lived, set to copy pages in the Ten Courts until the debt was paid. The sums were arranged so it never would be, and they still weigh and tax every stranger who passes.",
	"pool_crab": "The blood pools of the lowest court held the souls who could not pay, and the crabs were bred to pinch them so they never rested. The Court that filled the pools is gone, but the crabs, red as the water, know no other work and pinch whatever comes near.",
	"court_hound": "Bred in the Burning Court with coals for eyes, the hounds were set to run down any soul that fled before its judgement. Since the Wheel stopped more souls have fled than ever, and the hounds have run themselves half mad chasing them, the living included.",
	"thread_moth": "The Weaver's silver-thread moths came down the chimneys of Qingshi one warm spring night, each trailing a strand finer than hair. They stitch as they fly, roof to roof and door to door, and flutter at the eyes of whoever tries to cut their seams.",
	"paper_crow": "The Lady's threads folded the paper crows from whatever Qingshi had lying about: shrine prayer slips, pages of the children's copybooks. Some still bear a child's practice characters on their wings, and all of them dive at anyone who tries to leave.",
	"silk_bandit": "They were highwaymen of the hill roads who came to rob Qingshi in the confusion, until the Lady's silk wrapped their faces in the night. Now they sleepwalk at the ends of her threads, guarding the village they came to rob, and their masks do the dreaming.",
	"tea_beetle": "Jade tea beetles have always nibbled a leaf from every bush, and Lu Yu has always let them; he says the bitten leaves brew sweeter. The threads have made them sick and savage, and now they strip whole terraces bare and charge any picker who climbs.",
	"terrace_serpent": "Terrace serpents carry water down the hill, channel to channel, as the Dragon of the Nine Terraces taught them. With their master sewn asleep and the channels stitched shut, they writhe in the mud, thread-sick and thirsty, striking at every ankle.",
	"hill_sprite": "Cloud tea needs cloud, and the mist-hill sprites are the morning mists that wrap the high terraces until noon. The Weaver's threads have caught them like fish in a net, and a mist that cannot drift away turns cold, heavy and spiteful.",
	"silkworm": "Not Can Niang's silkworms but the Lady's, bred to spin a silk no farmer ever could: thread spun from souls. They eat mulberry leaves for a month, as all silkworms do, and then they must spin, and anything warm and living will serve.",
	"silk_moth": "Born without mouths, the Lady's mulberry moths cannot eat and will not live out the week. They spend those few days dusting the woods with her powder, until every path through the mulberries looks like a wall and travellers walk in circles.",
	"cocoon_wraith": "A thousand cocoons were boiled for every bolt of silk before the moths could break free. The Lady gave the ghosts of those that never hatched her thread for bodies; they guard the soul-cocoons and wrap intruders in the sleep they never woke from.",
	"spindle_imp": "Whittled from the Loom's offcuts to wind its thread, spindle imps twirl on one leg like the spindles they were cut from, and Zhi used to sweep up after them. Now they wind anything they can catch, and would gladly wind a traveller up with the rest.",
	"warp_wraith": "The Loom's warp threads run down to every border in the world below: a ford, a mountain pass, a gate. As the borders came undone, the loose ends rose back up the warp as wraiths, and they try to knot themselves round anything that will hold still.",
	"shuttle_hawk": "The shuttle hawks were hatched to carry the weft, darting through the warp so fast that the threads crackle with lightning behind them. To a shuttle hawk anything that crosses the warp is weft to be beaten into place, and travellers most of all.",
	"flood_carp": "When the Silver River spilled down the Sky Road, the star-carp came with it and woke in flooded paddies with mud in their gills and starlight on their scales. They thrash at anyone who wades near, trying to swim back up to a sky they can no longer see.",
	"reed_wisp": "Cold little lights that gather in the reeds wherever someone once waited to cross the ford and never did. The flood has fed them and the Widow's calling draws them close; they lead travellers out into the deep water, meaning only to show them the far bank.",
	"ferry_crab": "For ten thousand years these crabs clung to the stone posts where the Cowherd moors his ferry, until their shells grew as grey and hard as the stone. The flood tore the posts from the bank, and the crabs pinch anyone who comes near what is left of them.",
	"cloud_ape": "The apes of the cloud cliffs always lived a ledge below the magpies, eating the walnuts they dropped and envying their nests. When the storm scattered the flock they climbed up at last, and now they defend every stolen nest as if they had woven it.",
	"bridge_magpie": "Magpies flung to the four winds by the storm that broke the bridge, too dazed to find the heights again. They peck at every stranger, half in fear and half in hope, searching for the one feather of their own that would show them the way home.",
	"gale_wolf": "Grey wolves have always hunted the heights, but the storm on the night the river burst got into their blood. Now they run on the gale itself with lightning in their hackles, and chase anything with feathers off the edge of the cliffs.",
	"star_moth": "Small moths shaken from the wings of the great Moth of the Seven Stars, each carrying a speck of the Weaver's stolen light. They drink lantern flame and starshine alike, and the palace corridors grow dim wherever a swarm settles to feed.",
	"loom_wraith": "Every dawn and dusk the Weaver left unwoven when she stopped her loom got up off the warp by itself, grey and unfinished, and drifted out into the gardens. They are the colourless sunsets the Cowherd has been watching, looking for someone to finish them.",
	"star_qilin": "Young qilin of the palace herd, foaled from starlight and raised by the Qilin of the Weaving Hall to believe the Weaver must be guarded from the world. They charge any stranger smelling of the earth below, as they were taught, though nobody told them why.",
	"kunlun_tiger": "The snow tigers of Kunlun believe the Queen Mother is one of their own, and with her teeth and her temper they may be right. Like everything on the mountain they are older than they look, and they take every visitor for someone come to frighten her.",
	"jade_qilin": "Shaped by the Queen Mother from the green rim of the Jasper Pool and set to graze among the peach trees to keep thieves away. They have done it for ten thousand years, and not one of them has been allowed a peach, which has done nothing for their tempers.",
	"kunlun_peach_sprite": "A peach of Kunlun ripens once in three thousand years; one that nobody picks goes on ripening, and begins to hum. These are the peaches nobody came for, grown into small sweet-smelling sprites, and they flail at every picker, furious at being passed over.",
	"noon_cur": "The market dogs of Yanggu used to sleep in the shade all afternoon and bark at the moon all night. After nine days of noon with no shade and no moon, their shadows crept under their feet and would not come out, and the dogs went mad looking for them.",
	"noon_cicada": "A cicada waits seven years underground for one summer of song. These came up into the longest summer ever made and have sung through all nine days of it without stopping, louder and wilder, as if they know that when it ends it will take them with it.",
	"clay_toad": "When the wells of Yanggu sank, the toads dug into the last of the mud to wait for rain, as toads always have. The rain never came; the sun baked them into clay, and they climbed out as cracked pottery, guarding the dry wells as if they were still full.",
	"plague_locust": "Each was once a green grasshopper of the millet plains, living alone, until the drought crowded them onto the last living stalks. Crowded locusts change colour, then they change their minds, and then there is only the swarm and its one enormous appetite.",
	"drought_scorpion": "Scorpions have always slept deep under Hou Ji's fields, where the ground stays cool. When nine days of noon split the earth to a man's depth, they woke blinking in the glare, and now they guard every crack as if the plain had been broken open to rob them.",
	"haze_wisp": "The shimmer that hangs over the dyke road at noon has had nine days of noon to grow in, and it has grown a will. It shows the thirsty a lake just ahead and another just beyond, and leads them off the road towards water that is always a little further on.",
	"spring_macaque": "The macaques of Fusang have bathed in its hot springs since Xihe washed her ten children there, and still tell their young about it as if they had held the towels. Since the springs began to boil they guard the last bearable pools with teeth and pebbles.",
	"sun_fledgling": "Little three-legged crows who ride the sun across the sky and roost in the Fusang by night. Jinwu's terror has run through the whole roost; they have not dared leave the crown for nine days, and they dive shrieking at anyone who climbs with a weapon.",
	"fusang_sprite": "Every leaf of the great mulberry remembers the morning an archer stood on the ridge and nine suns fell burning from its branches. This week's fallen leaves have risen as scorched, quick sprites, and they fly at anyone carrying a bow, or anything like one.",
	"frost_hare": "White hares of the moon's frost, cousins of the Jade Rabbit, who have begged for centuries to help pound the elixir and are never let near the mortar. Now they have made themselves its guards, and thump at anyone who smells of the archer's valley.",
	"moon_toad": "Three-legged toads lived in the moon's cold pools long before Chang'e came, and some songs claim she turned into one, which they have never forgiven. Since the night grew long they gulp the moonlight down and hoard it, afraid the dark will take the rest.",
	"silver_crane": "On the Mid-Autumn night the silver cranes of Guanghan carried the immortals down to the festivals below and home by dawn. Now the night will not end and the festival never comes, and they wheel round the moon, stabbing at anything that crosses their flight.",
	"gate_carp": "Golden carp who swam up from the sea to leap the Dragon Gate, as their kind have since Yu cut the gorge, and found the river running backwards at the top. They mill below the falls in a sulking, snapping crowd, and every failed jump costs a golden scale.",
	"eel_bandit": "Black Eel's gang: river eels with a rat's appetite who used to live off whatever the ferries dropped. The flood taught them that everything floats, and now they slither through the drowned warehouses of Longmen with sacks in their teeth, calling it salvage.",
	"silt_crab": "Crabs of the quay mud who wear shells of packed river silt. Since the river began running backwards in its sleep it has dumped its silt on the quays instead of the sea, and the crabs have grown fat and quarrelsome on it, pinching at boots like toll-keepers.",
	"flood_toad": "Daughters of the Toad Who Swallowed the Dykes, hatched in the paddies with their mother's appetite. Each gulps a little of the flood and will not give it back, and they squat on the dyke paths with bulging throats, sure that water kept is water saved.",
	"mud_heron": "Herons of the drowned fields who have joined the Heron Lord's court, each with a twist of rice-straw round its neck for a sash. They collect his tax of one fish per fish with great solemnity and keep all of it, which, as their lord says, is what a tax is.",
	"rice_wraith": "Nine years of harvests drowned in these paddies behind Gun's walls before Yu came, and the rice that never was did not forget it. When the water rose again it stood up from the fields as pale stalks in the shape of farmers, still looking for the sickle.",
	"slag_golem": "Four thousand years of casting have heaped slag round the pits of Tushan: all the bronze that ran off the moulds and never became a bell or a cauldron. When the pits woke, the slag stood up too, hot and lumpy, wanting a shape of its own.",
	"bronze_hound": "Tushan put a little taotie face on every vessel it cast, as a warning against greed. When the great Taotie woke in the casting yard, every small face on every pot and bell for a li around woke hungry with it, grew legs, and went looking for food.",
	"charcoal_wisp": "Tushan's charcoal-burners bank their pits with turf and leave them to smoulder for days, and say a pit left too long grows a wisp: a little blue flame that walks. Since the casting pits woke, the woods are full of them, wandering off to light the trees.",
	"flood_wolf": "Wolves of black water that run down every river Gonggong is pulling home, herding the current uphill like sheep. Wherever they pass, the rivers turn and flow backwards behind them, and they snap at anyone standing on the bank in the way.",
	"pillar_sentinel": "When Gonggong broke Mount Buzhou its stones were scattered round the lake, and every one of them remembered holding up the sky. They have stood guard over the stump ever since, cracked and stubborn, still braced for a weight that is no longer there.",
	"kun_fry": "Young of the Kun of the Northern Darkness, each no bigger than a fishing boat, who followed their parent up the rivers into the black lake believing it was the sea. Any one of them may be a Peng one day, and they sting as if they knew it.",
	"cloud_kite": "Fork-tailed kites that nest in the crown of the Jianmu and ride the updrafts off the cloud sea. The Feathered Folk's wind bells always kept them wary of the aeries; with the bells torn away and the winds running wild, they circle the Egg-Nursery, waiting.",
	"thunder_mote": "In the Rain Ministry's day every spark of thunder had a cloud to follow and a field to announce. With no rain signed for, the sparks have nothing to follow; they drift loose over the islands, crackling, and leap at anything tall, hoping it is a cloud.",
	"rain_serpent": "Little rain-dragons of the Ministry, each given a page of the ledger and a cloud to carry to the place it named. Since nobody signs the pages they coil over Juntian, heavy with rain and nowhere to put it, and lash out at anyone who looks like a clerk.",
	"gate_lion": "The gilded lions that flank the Gate of Heaven sat still for ten thousand years while the Court came and went. With nothing left to guard, they have woken restless and prowl the islands; one carried a fox's key in its mouth for a month without noticing.",
	"fox_wisp": "Foxfire is what a celestial fox's laughter looks like when nobody is there to hear it. The foxes of Changhe have laughed at the Gate for six hundred years, and the leftover jokes drift about the islands as little cold flames, tricking travellers.",
	"cloud_soldier": "Soldiers of the Court's gate guard, posted on Changhe with orders to let no one through and no word of relief. Nobody told them the Court had fallen, and they would not believe it; they stand at their posts in armour of cloud worn thin as mist.",
	"root_worm": "Pale worms who lived for ages in the deep dark with nothing to eat but stone. When the Jianmu's roots came down among them, sweet with sap and pinched between the tree and Li, they found the first meal of their lives, and they will not give it up.",
	"blindfish": "Carp who swam down with the rivers into the earth long ago and never found the way back up. Down here there is no Dragon Gate to leap and nothing to look at, so over the generations they gave up their eyes; they hunt by the sound of a heartbeat in the water.",
	"glow_crab": "Cave crabs who glow because they grind the Pearl-Weepers' fallen tears into their shells. With the Torch Dragon's eyes shut, their own light has become precious to them, and they guard it with claws and a cold blue flare from anything that comes near.",
	"ghost_soldier": "Shades of soldiers from every war the earth has buried, who came down to Youdu still waiting to be dismissed and took to patrolling the streets because nobody ever did. Since the rolls scattered they challenge every passer-by, living or dead, for a tally.",
	"paper_horse": "Old Zhi Ma folds the paper horses that nobody up above remembered to burn, so that no shade need walk. One of them, folded a little too well, decided it had not been made to be ridden, and it gallops through the Dark Capital, being made of paper at people.",
	"lantern_shade": "In Youdu every lantern stands for a shade still waiting to go up to the Wheel. When the roots heaved the city and the rolls scattered, some shades, afraid of being forgotten, crawled into their own lanterns, and they flare at anyone who tries to snuff them.",
	"mirage_wisp": "When the Court's mirage lifted from Penglai after two thousand years, not all of it went. Scraps linger over the bare shore: a pale tower, a harbour of ships, a face from Qin, looking for something to hide and lunging at anyone who sees through them.",
	"stranded_shark": "Sharks of the deep channels who chased the drain too far and woke on sand when the sea went out from under them. They lie in the last warm puddles, drying, and snap at the children of Landfall who come to look, as frightened as anything on the shore.",
	"sand_crab": "The drain left the sandbars bare, and their crabs suddenly inherited a great deal of property. They pinch at anyone who crosses it; one of them, according to little Haibao of Landfall, wears an old Qin coin on its back and answers to the name of Emperor.",
	"cinnabar_toad": "Toads that lived for centuries in the warm ash under Fangzhang's furnaces, eating the cinnabar spilled from the crucibles until it coloured them through. When the furnaces went cold they crawled out into the herb beds, scarlet and cross, looking for heat.",
	"elixir_crane": "The cranes of Fangzhang carried the immortals between the isles for two thousand years, paid in the crumbs of their pills. With the furnaces cold and the immortals going nowhere, they help themselves now, and stab at anyone between them and a pill-box.",
	"herb_rabbit": "Distant cousins of the Jade Rabbit, who have nibbled the immortals' herb terraces for centuries and grown long-lived and very round on them. Now the garden is dying they are eating as fast as they can, and they fight anyone who looks as if he wants a share.",
	"mist_phantom": "The mist of Yingzhou has hidden the isle so long that it remembers the ships it turned away, every sail that came seeking the immortals and lost its way. Those memories walk the cliffs as phantoms, frightening the turtles with faces the sea took long ago.",
	"bore_horse": "White horses of foam that run before the tidal bore, carrying Wu Zixu's anger up the rivers twice a day. With no sea to raise a tide they have bolted over the bare sand, wild-eyed, trampling all in their way as if they could gallop the water back.",
	"shell_soldier": "The river people say the tide is an army and Wu Zixu its general; these are his soldiers, drowned men and crabs in shell armour who marched up the estuaries behind him for ages. The tide has stopped, and they stand in ranks on the sand, awaiting orders.",
	"drowned_sailor": "Sailors of Xu Fu's fleet who went down on the slope of the Ruins and were tumbled in the dark for two thousand years. Now the water has gone they walk the sea floor stiff-legged, hauling on ropes that are not there, and attack anyone not on the crew list.",
	"abyss_eel": "Eels from the walls of Guixu, who spent ages swimming against the water that pours for ever into the dark. Now the sea is falling past them faster than ever, they have climbed out onto the bare slope, savage with the long effort of not being swept away.",
	"ruin_sentinel": "Stone guardians of the gold palaces of Daiyu and Yuanqiao, the isles that sank when the giant took their turtles. They kept their gates under the sea for ages, and now the water has gone they stand in the open air, guarding doors with nobody behind them.",
	"jiuli_warrior": "Farmers of the Nine Li who took up their war god's bronze when the harvests failed and the river was not theirs. They died in the hundred days of fog, and the formation has woken them still at war; they fight as men fight for their children's supper.",
	"zhuolu_fog": "A shred of the hundred-day fog Chiyou breathed over Zhuolu, which Yinglong drained into channels and the Court sealed in the lock. Woken with the Eight Gates, it creeps over the land again, and those who walk into it cannot find their own feet.",
	"bronze_brow": "Younger kin of Chiyou's eighty-one brothers, bronze-browed and fed on sand and stone, who held the rear at Zhuolu while their elders held the line. Woken with the formation, they march with the war-band, chewing gravel and asking everyone what day it is.",
	"fetter_sapling": "Seedlings of the first maples, which grew from the fetters struck off Chiyou and are red with his blood. Woken with the formation, they walk the funeral road on their roots, and close their branches round travellers' ankles like the shackles they once were.",
	"banner_ember": "Sparks shed by the Red Comet as it came down to the Gate of Sight, each still burning with a little of the Banner's grief. Look into one and you will see a small war of your own: a quarrel never mended, a door slammed, a letter left unanswered.",
	# ---- Part XIV: the grudges of the whole journey
	"grudge_wisp": "When a foe falls with a grudge it cannot carry over the Naihe Bridge, the grudge is left behind at the Wheel, a small hot coal of being wronged. This year they have learned to float up, and they burn whoever reminds them of the one who wronged them.",
	"debt_shade": "Every promise made to the dead and never kept leaves a shade standing at the Wheel's rim with an empty hand out. They are not angry. They are only waiting to be paid, and they will wait inside anyone who walks too near.",
	"karma_moth": "Karma moths are what the Wheel makes of small debts: a borrowed coin, a word not said, a door not held. They are drawn to the warmth of anyone who owes, and their wings are made of tallies, each one a little thing that was never put right.",
	# ---- Part XV (the Lotus release): the things that eat forgetting
	"loose_thread": "When the old weave of the Lotus Seal began to come apart, the threads that came loose did not lie still. They drift about the graves in pink coils, looking for the pattern they belonged to, and wind themselves round anything warm until it forgets its own shape.",
	"forgetting_moth": "Grey moths with no faces, woken by the Warden of the Lock to eat what the Founder pressed. They feed on remembering the way other moths feed on wool: a name here, a summer there, until only the holes are left. They are drawn to lamps, and cannot forget it.",
	# Part XVI (the Living Land release)
	"lean_month": "The last month of a hard year has a hungry ghost of its own: the Lean Month, who walks the granaries counting what is left and shaking its head. It is grey and thin as a stalk of millet in the snow, and wherever it passes the rice jars ring hollow.",
	"candle_moth": "Candle-eating moths come out of the dark at the year's end and drink the flames off lamp wicks the way other moths drink nectar. Xi breeds them in the cold places to clear the way: a street with no lamps lit is a street the Last Night can walk down.",
	"nian_cub": "Nian is the year, and every year leaves a little of itself behind: a fear never quite got over, a worry never quite put down. They curl up in the hills and grow into cubs with green scales and red manes, and every New Year's Eve they follow the big one down to the villages to learn how to frighten people.",
	# Part XVIII (the New Fire release): what gets into a tired fire, the cinders, and the fire's children
	"damp_smoke": "A fire in good heart drives the damp out of a boat; a tired one lets it in. It comes up out of the bilge as a grey wisp that smells of wet rope, settles on the coals like a hen on eggs, and sits there until they are cold. The river women blow them off, night after night.",
	"bilge_imp": "Every boat has a little water in the bottom that never quite goes, and every bilge has something living in it that the boat-wives do not discuss. While the stern-pot burns bright it keeps to its place. This month it has been climbing out.",
	"drowned_ember": "When a boat goes down with its fire alight, the last coal goes out in the river, and the river folk say it does not forgive it. It walks the moorings on wet nights in the shape of somebody looking for a light, with a red spark where its heart was.",
	"ash_moth": "Moths come to a flame and burn. On the Ashen Volcano there are moths that came to the first fire and burned so long ago that they have forgotten they are dead: wings of grey flake, a body of cinder, and a longing for anything that is still alight.",
	"cinder_imp": "The imps of the crater were sparks once, thrown out of Zhurong's forge and too proud to go out. They went black instead. They hate a live coal the way a poor relation hates a wedding, and they cannot keep away from one.",
	"clinker_crab": "Clinker is the glass a fire leaves when it burns badly, and on the volcano it has had ten thousand years to learn to walk. It walks sideways. Under the black shell there is a little heat left, which is all it has, and it will fight for more.",
	"wildfire_sprite": "A spark that got away. By itself it is nothing: it lands, it catches, it laughs, it is put out by a boot. But this summer they are the children of six bonfires that ran together, and each of them remembers being told that it was the proper fire and the others were not.",
	"spark_swallow": "The swallows nest under every eave in the land, and where a roof burns they fly out of it alight. The country people say a swallow with fire on its wings is only trying to get home, and that is exactly what makes it dangerous: it flies straight for the thatch.",
	"smoke_hound": "Where the wildfire has been, the smoke lies in the hollows, and in a dry summer wind it gets up and runs: low, grey, tireless, with two red points in front where the fire shows through. It runs ahead of the flames and finds them the way.",
	# Part XVII (the Spring Thunder release): the cold that comes back, and what the first thunder woke
	"rime_wisp": "On the clearest, coldest nights the breath of the sleeping land freezes in the air and does not fall: it drifts, white and glittering, and where it touches a leaf the leaf is furred with rime by morning. In a spring that will not come, the wisps forget to melt, and come down off the snow looking for anything warm to settle on.",
	"hoarfrost_hare": "The white hares of the high snow change their coats with the season, brown in summer and white in winter, and in the long cold this year they forgot how to change back. They have grown hoarfrost instead of fur, and where they run the frost runs with them, down out of Frostpeak and into the fields.",
	"late_frost": "Every spring the cold comes back once more, on a clear night just after the plum has flowered, and kills whatever came out too early: the farmers call it the cold that comes back, and say it is a widow who cannot believe the winter is over. This year there are a great many of her, and they are all walking down the terraces.",
	"waking_scorpion": "The scorpions sleep curled in the cracks of the ground all winter, and the first thunder of spring wakes them, hungry and bad-tempered, which is why old women hang mugwort on the door at Jingzhe. These ones slept in the cracks the nine days of noon split in the plains, and woke all at once.",
	"waking_centipede": "A centipede with a hundred red legs and a temper to match, one of the five poisons. They sleep under stones all winter and come out at the first thunder; this year the thunder was the loudest anybody remembers, and they came out in a hurry.",
	"waking_toad": "Not the lucky three-legged toad with a coin in its mouth: the other kind, with warts full of poison and eyes like lamps, one of the five poisons the thunder wakes. They were sleeping in the mud of the old riverbeds under the millet, and the thunder brought them up out of it in hundreds.",
	"unweaver_shuttle": "Weavers' shuttles from the Loom of Borders that the Unweaver has turned round, so that instead of carrying the thread through the warp they pull it back out. They scuttle on their bobbins like crabs, and where they have been, cloth becomes yarn again.",
}

const BOSS := {
	# ---- Part XXII: The High Roads
	"shanxiao": ["The old records of the seasons know it: in the deep mountains of the west there are people a foot or so high, with one leg, who are not afraid of men and come to travellers' fires by night to roast crabs and steal salt. Whoever offends one falls sick of a fever. They are called shanxiao, and they fear only the crack of bamboo thrown on a fire.",
		"That is why bamboo was burned at the New Year, long before there was powder to put in it: the first firecrackers were for this creature. Later tellers add that it speaks like a man and calls travellers by name, and that one must not answer; and that a house which leaves it salt, and does not laugh at its foot, has its wood carried down in the night for nothing."],
	"fei_lian": ["Fei Lian is the Earl of the Wind: a body like a stag's, a head like a sparrow's with horns, a serpent's tail and a leopard's markings, and in the Li Sao the poet sends him running behind the chariot as a rear-guard. He keeps the winds in a bag. At Zhuolu he and the Master of Rain raised a storm for Chiyou against the Yellow Emperor, and lost.",
		"Of Master Lie the Zhuangzi says that he rode the wind, cool and skilful, and was gone fifteen days before he came back; that such a man is rare; and that although he was spared the trouble of walking, he still had something he must wait for. Nobody thought to ask what the wind made of being waited for."],
	"shan_gui": ["There seems to be someone in the fold of the mountain, in a coat of fig leaves with a belt of hanging moss: so begins the ninth of the Nine Songs. She rides a red leopard, and striped lynxes follow. She has come to meet somebody. She says: I live in the dark bamboo and never see the sky; the way was hard and perilous, and I alone am come late.",
		"He is not there. She stands alone on the top of the mountain with the clouds rolling under her feet, and the east wind brings the rain. She says he thinks of her but has no time; then that he thinks of her, but doubts. The song ends without his arriving: thunder rumbles, rain darkens, the gibbons mourn all night, and she thinks of her lord and meets with sorrow only."],
	"dongyue": ["Of the five great mountains the eastern is the eldest. Seventy-two rulers, Guan Zhong told his duke, had gone up it to perform the Feng and the Shan, by which a king reports to Heaven and Earth that he has succeeded. The First Emperor went up in a storm and gave a title to the pine he sheltered under; Emperor Wu set up a stele there with nothing on it at all.",
		"The mountain's own lord keeps the registers of the living and the dead, and receives. Mount Tai does not refuse a clod of earth, it is said, and so is high. Confucius climbed it and found the world small. A poet stood below it and promised himself that one day he would stand on the very top and see every other mountain little. Nobody says whether the mountain was asked."],
	# Part XIX (the Hundred Works release)
	"wooden_magpie": ["In the book of Master Mo it is written that Gongshu Ban cut bamboo and wood and made a magpie, and when it was finished it flew, and for three days it did not come down. He thought it the height of skill.",
		"Master Mo said to him: your making a magpie is not equal to a carpenter's making a linchpin. In a moment he cuts a piece of wood three inches long, and it bears a load of fifty stone. What benefits people is called skilful; what does not benefit them is called clumsy.",
		"The book does not say what became of the magpie. Nobody who has made a thing that flew for three days has ever believed that it came down."],
	"cloud_ladder": ["The cloud ladder was Gongshu Ban's masterpiece and his shame: a tower on wheels with a ladder that hinged out over a city wall, built for the King of Chu to take the little state of Song.",
		"Mozi walked ten days and nights to the king's court, took off his belt for a wall, and with nine slips of wood turned nine assaults on a tabletop. Ban said he knew one more way to win, and would not say it. Mozi said he knew what it was, and that three hundred of his students were already on the walls of Song. The king called off the war.",
		"This one has no king. The engines of the land, which have been taught to want, wanted a thing to build, and of everything Ban ever drew this is the one that only goes up."],
	"the_player": ["In the book of Master Lie it is written that King Mu of Zhou, coming home from the west, was shown by an artificer named Yan Shi a figure that walked, sang in tune and danced in time, so that the king took it for a living man. At the end of the performance it winked at the royal concubines.",
		"The king, in a rage, would have put Yan Shi to death. The artificer at once took the figure apart: leather, wood, glue and lacquer, with a liver, a heart, lungs and kidneys all of them made. Without the heart it could not speak; without the liver it could not see; without the kidneys it could not walk.",
		"The king sighed and said: can human skill be the equal of the Maker of Things? The book does not say that Yan Shi kept the heart, or what became of a dancer who had been shown, in front of a court, exactly what it was. It has had three thousand years to think about the wink."],
	"jixin": ["Jixin means the machine heart. The words are an old gardener's, in the book of the Master of the Lacquer Garden: where there are machines there will be machine affairs, and where there are machine affairs there will be a machine heart.",
		"With a machine heart in the breast, he said, what is pure and simple is spoiled, the spirit is unsettled, and the Way will not carry you. He did not say machines were wicked. He said he would be ashamed.",
		"The first master of the oldest pit at Tushan cast the Nine Cauldrons for Yu the Great, and when they were done there was a bell wanted, and when the bell was done, a tripod. He was a good man and a great founder, and he never once said: enough. The pit is where what he became has waited for every escapement in the land to come home to it."],
	# Part XXI (the Hundred Arms release)
	"yuan_gong": ["In the Spring and Autumn of Wu and Yue, the King of Yue asked for somebody to teach his army the sword, and was told of a maiden of the southern forest. On her way north she met an old man who called himself Master Yuan and asked to see her art.",
		"He pulled down a stalk of bamboo; the tip broke off and fell, and she caught it before it touched the ground. He swung the butt at her; she met it three times and struck back, and he flew up into a tree and was a white ape, and was gone.",
		"The book goes on to her audience with the king and has no more to say about the ape. It does not seem to have occurred to anybody to wonder what he made of it, or what an ape does with the only lesson it was ever given."],
	"sword_dragons": ["Gan Jiang of Wu was ordered to cast two swords for his king. He gathered the iron of five mountains and the gold of the six directions, and for three months the metal would not melt. His wife Mo Ye cut her hair and her nails and cast them into the furnace; some say she cast in herself. Then it ran.",
		"He named the swords for the two of them and gave the king only one, and died for it. Long after, a man carrying the pair across the ford at Yanping felt them leap from his belt into the river, and when he looked down there were two dragons in the water, each several fathoms long, and then nothing.",
		"The histories record it as an omen. They record what the swords were made of, who was killed for them, and which constellations they shone under. None of them says whether the two dragons, once they were in the water, ever found each other."],
	"king_zhao": ["In the Discourse on Swords, King Wen of Zhao kept three thousand swordsmen at his gate, and they fought before him day and night until more than a hundred were killed or wounded in a year, and he never wearied of it. After three years the state was in decline, and the other lords were making plans.",
		"A man in swordsman's dress came and offered him three swords. The sword of the Son of Heaven has the valley of Yan for its point and Mount Tai for its edge, and when it is used the world submits. The sword of a lord has brave men for its point and wise men for its hilt. The sword of the common man cuts throats, and is no different from a fighting cock.",
		"The king led him up into the hall and walked three times round the table and could not sit. He did not leave his palace for three months. And the swordsmen, the book says in its last line, as if it were good news, all killed themselves in their quarters."],
	"wuzhu": ["Weapons are instruments of ill omen; there are things that detest them, and so one who has the Way does not abide in them. At home the gentleman honours the left; in war, the right. Arms are not the gentleman's tools: he uses them when there is no help for it, and holds calm and restraint the best.",
		"He does not find victory beautiful. To find it beautiful is to delight in the killing of men, and one who delights in killing will never have his way with the world. When many have been killed, weep for them with grief and sorrow; when the battle is won, keep it as one keeps a funeral.",
		"The old book speaks of weapons as things that are used, well or badly. It has nothing to say about a weapon that nobody made and nobody holds. Wuzhu is only the word for that: without a master. It is what is written on a grave when no family comes."],
	# Part XX (the Hollow Hills release)
	"fen_yang": ["In the Discourses of the States it is written that Ji Huanzi of Lu dug a well and found something like an earthen jar, with a thing in it like a sheep. He sent to ask Confucius, saying it was a dog, to test him.",
		"Confucius said: by what I have heard, it is a sheep. The prodigies of wood and stone are the kui and the wangliang; the prodigies of water are the dragon and the wangxiang; the prodigy of earth is the fenyang.",
		"That is all the book says. It does not say what the men of Lu did with it, or whether anybody ever thought to ask what a lamb was doing asleep in a jar under a field, or whose jar it was."],
	"white_rat": ["In the Journey to the West the lady of the Bottomless Cave on Hollow Mountain is a rat with a gold nose and white hair, three hundred years in cultivation, who once stole the flowers and candles from the Buddha's own altar.",
		"She was let off, and took the Heavenly King Li for a father and his son Nezha for a brother, and kept a tablet to them both in her parlour with incense burning before it. When she was caught a second time, it was the tablet that saved her.",
		"The book is hard on her. But it admits that her cave was three hundred li round, that her table was well kept, and that nobody found the way into it who was not shown."],
	"wangliang": ["In the book of the Master of the Lacquer Garden, Penumbra said to Shadow: a little while ago you were walking and now you are standing still; a little while ago you were sitting and now you are standing up. Why this lack of independence?",
		"Shadow said: do I have to wait for something before I can be like this? Does what I wait for also have to wait for something? Am I waiting for the scales of a snake or the wings of a cicada? How do I know why it is so? How do I know why it is not so?",
		"Penumbra is wangliang: the faint edge round a shadow, the thing that depends on what depends. It is also the old name for a spirit of rocks and hollow places that mimics the human voice. Nobody recorded whether Penumbra was content with Shadow's answer."],
	"xuanpin": ["The valley spirit never dies: it is called the dark female. The gate of the dark female is called the root of heaven and earth. It is there within us all the while; draw upon it as you will, it never runs dry.",
		"Thirty spokes share one hub: it is where there is nothing that the cart is of use. Clay is kneaded to make a pot: it is where there is nothing that the pot is of use. Doors and windows are cut to make a room: it is where there is nothing that the room is of use.",
		"The old book never says she can be driven out. It did not occur to the old man who wrote it that a whole land, all meaning well, might fill in its hollow places one at a time, and then stand about wondering where the water had gone."],
	"jade_mantis": ["The Jade Mantis Matriarch laid her egg-cases among the spirit herbs, for a mantis hatched among them was born with qi in her blood. Ten years before, a herb-picking cultivator had trodden a season of them underfoot; she killed him, and kept the groves ever after.", "This spring's egg-cases hung swaying in the grove behind her, and every herb a sect boy had pulled was one less for her daughters. She did not understand mercy, only seasons, and she meant to see this one through."],
	"iron_fang": ["Iron Fang was a farmer's son who climbed the Azure Cloud steps at twelve and was sent down again the same day for want of a spirit root. He lost a tooth in the scuffle at the gate, had it replaced with iron, and swore that the sect would one day pay for both.", "Years later he ran the bamboo road with forty crossbows. When the Blood Moon offered spirit stones and a gourd of marsh venom for the sect's wells, he took both; the healer he caught to pour it refused, and so he kept her bound."],
	"bamboo_spirit": ["Older than the bandits, older than the Court, the Bamboo Spirit was the forest itself, one root under ten thousand stems. On a night of red moon long ago, Xu Mo's hunters killed a fleeing bearer of the Nine Heavens root there, and the roots drank what was spilled.", "With the blood they drank the moon's command. When the moon reddened again, the command woke in the roots and turned the old spirit against the very root it had once failed to shelter; it wailed as it fought, and wanted to be stopped."],
	"bog_colossus": ["It began as a dyke: a wall of black mud a hundred paces long, raised by the settlers the Court sent to drain the Crimson Marsh. When the red water took them, the Marsh Hag poured poison qi into their wall and stood it up as her doorkeeper.", "It had been built to hold the water back and keep people safe, and now it held nothing. The Bog Colossus knew only mud, and hunger, and a dim, heavy sense that it had once been asked to protect someone."],
	"marsh_hag": ["Nobody remembered her name. She had been herb-wife to the settlers the Court sent into the marsh, the only one who could coax blood lotuses out of the red water, and when the settlers drowned and nobody came for her cures, she learned that poison paid better.", "She sold venom to bandits and news of every bright young root to the Blood Moon, and she fed on the cultivators who came for her flowers. Yet she kept the lotuses, every one, though she had nobody left to heal."],
	"ice_empress": ["Because she had never been warm, the Ice Empress once let a jade-cutter with a lantern into her court. He grew old and died in what felt to her like a single winter, and she sealed the caverns so that no warmth would ever come in again to be lost.", "Under the red moon that rose over the north-east, Xu Mo offered her a winter that would never have to end. She took it, and she hated every traveller's warmth for reminding her of the lantern."],
	"frost_wyrm": ["A river-dragon of the eastern valleys was asleep in its pool when the Ice Empress's winter stopped ending. The ice closed over it, and it woke inside a glacier with its brood curled round it in the cold: the Frost Wyrm.", "Xu Mo, who had given the Empress her endless winter, came to the wyrm under the same red moon and promised it spring if it would guard the caverns for him. It guarded them, hating every warm thing that passed, and waited for a thaw that was never coming."],
	"storm_ape_king": ["Three hundred years ago a young ape watched a swordsman die on a crag of Thunder Valley with his blade raised to the storm, and never forgot how the lightning seemed to bow to him.", "Grown into the Storm Ape King, he bent every sword that came into the valley, looking for one that would not bend. None ever had; it made him loud, and lonely, and very hard to hit."],
	"thunder_roc": ["An egg lay on the highest crag of Thunder Valley for a hundred years, struck by every storm that passed, until the lightning broke it open. The Thunder Roc never knew a mother, only thunder, and it called the storm its own the way a child calls a blanket its own.", "The sword ghost had watched it grow from the rock below, and he did not ask for its death lightly; but a feather struck by a hundred years of lightning was what the failing Seal needed to sing again."],
	"blood_hound": ["Of all the creatures in the Blood Moon Palace, the Crimson Hellhound was the only one Xu Mo ever fed from his own hand. Each of its three heads had a name, and he was the only one who used them.", "His huntsmen ran it on every trail that smelled of the root. Sixteen winters before, in a gorge of maples, two strangers had led it away from a crying child on purpose; it never knew what it had missed, but some nights it woke with that scent in all three noses."],
	"general_blood": ["Xue Sha was a famine orphan, sucking a stone for the taste of it, on the night Xu Mo found him and gave him the moon's blood to drink. He never thirsted for anything else again, and he never forgot that every drop was only lent.", "He despised the mortal-born because he had been one and remembered being hungry, and a sweeper walking into his lord's hall reminded him too much of the boy in the famine."],
	"general_shadow": ["There was only one thing Mo Ying had feared as a hired blade, and that was being caught. He sold his body to the Blood Moon so that no hand and no sword could ever touch him again, and Xu Mo made him a general of shadows.", "The bargain held. Nothing had touched him for two hundred years: not a blade, not a friend, not the rain. Only light still reached him, and he hated it for being the last thing in the world that could."],
	"demon_lord": ["For three hundred years Xu Mo hunted the bearers of the Nine Heavens root, and he always found them before the fifth realm. His servants said he came home from every hunt alone, shut the doors of the throne hall, and that the moon stayed redder for a month.", "He believed, or had made himself believe, that one more root would put an end to something far worse than himself. He had believed it nineteen times, and he meant to believe it once more."],
	"crab_general": ["No order of the Dragon King's had ever been questioned at the Coral Gate; its crab general had held it for eight hundred years by obeying. When the orders began to come wrong, cold and strange and smelling of the rift, he knew the King was not himself.", "He obeyed anyway, because to him loyalty that chose its orders was no loyalty at all; and behind his visor he prayed for someone strong enough to crack his shell and go and find out what was wrong."],
	"tide_hydra": ["In better days the Tide Hydra was a single serpent with a single head, driving the tides for the Dragon King as a herdsman drives cattle. When the rift seized the King, every tide he could no longer hold back grew it another head, and every head another hunger.", "By the time Lin Feng came there were nine, and they quarrelled over everything except what to eat. Somewhere beneath them was a serpent that only wanted to go back to herding the sea."],
	"siren_queen": ["The Siren Queen's people lived so deep beneath the Dragon Palace that they knew the sun only from old songs. When a voice came down through the rift and promised them a sun of their own, she sang the King's guards out of their wits for it, one by one.", "The voice was the Emperor's, and the sun never came. She sang on all the same, because a song was the only sun she had ever been given, and she meant for travellers to drown in it with her."],
	"dragon_king": ["The Azure Dragon King had held the tide over the rift beneath his palace since before the sect was founded, as he had promised his father he would. The rift's grip closed on his heart slowly, a scale at a time, and he felt every one.", "While he could still speak he bade his envoy wait at the gate for whoever the old prophecy had promised, then spent what was left of himself holding the sea back from the coast. He did not want to fight the boy who came; he wanted to be stopped."],
	"lava_titan": ["A hill on the volcano's lower slope, warm at the core and content for ten thousand years: that was the Lava Titan, until Chi Yan woke it with a drop of Blood Moon fire and told it that it was a giant now.", "What a giant was for, it did not understand, except breaking; so it broke whatever was near, the old road and the stone shrines and anyone who came up the slope, sorrowfully, like a child who has just been told it is strong."],
	"cinder_behemoth": ["Fire-sages once worked forges in the volcano's heart, long before Huo Rong, and the Cinder Behemoth was their bellows-beast, a patient creature that breathed on their fires for a thousand years. When the last sage left, it lay down in the cold forge to wait.", "Nobody came back. Its own fire guttered, and despair hollowed it out like a burnt log; by the time the Phoenix went mad it would have eaten any flame at all, even the one inside a traveller."],
	"ash_phoenix": ["Once in every age the Ash Phoenix rose from her own ashes in the volcano and flew to the edge of the sky to see it whole. In this age she found it cracked and burning where the Emperor had fed on it, and she caught fire with it and could not stop.", "Unable to finish dying, and so unable to begin again, she burned on in agony while the Emperor pulled her strings through the pain. She knew it, and she wanted someone to put her out."],
	"inferno_lord": ["Chi Yan was the lamplighter of the Blood Moon Palace, a minor demon who kept its braziers burning for three hundred years, and when Xu Mo fell he was the last flame still lit. He did not grieve; he went over his dead master's head, to the one above him.", "The Emperor promised him the volcano for a body and heaven for a reward. He poured himself into the mountain and found he could never leave it again; every vent breathed when he breathed, and heaven never sent for him."],
	"rift_weaver": ["The runt of a brood hatched at the Origin of the Dao, the Rift Weaver was sent north with a single thread of its mother's web. In the Void Rift it found a patron at last: the Emperor admired its work and set it to spin the thin places of the world thinner.", "Through its webs the stolen heavens drained away like wine through muslin, and the Weaver was proud of every strand. It only ever wanted more thread, and bright souls made the brightest."],
	"star_devourer": ["Harmless and slow, the Star Devourer once grazed on the dust between the stars like a jellyfish in a summer sea. When the Emperor drained the heavens the dust ran out, and in its hunger it began on the stars themselves.", "It swallowed a dozen constellations, and shepherds and sailors below lost their way home. Still the hunger never left, and the light of a living soul smelled to it like dawn after a very long night."],
	"void_sentinel": ["The last mountain to fall into the rift became the Void Sentinel, and on its heart the Emperor wrote a single decree: none shall pass while I stand. Nothing else was ever written there.", "It stood between the drifting islands for an age, loving its decree as a hermit loves silence. It had never wondered what would happen if it fell, because it had never once imagined falling."],
	"chaos_serpent": ["A strand of the chaos from before the world slipped in through the thinning land and found that it could feed on doubt; that was the Chaos Serpent. The Emperor promised it a bearer's light for a crown and set it coiled across the road to the Celestial Gate.", "Hermit Wu Ji sat by its tail for a thousand years trying to decide whether to strike, and the serpent grew fat on every moment that he hesitated."],
	"heaven_general": ["Born above the clouds, General Tian Wei never once set foot on the earth. He swore his sword to the Heavens themselves, not to any throne, and when the Emperor sat down on heaven's throne he told himself it came to the same thing.", "For a thousand years he held the Gate against the mortal-born, because a general who doubted his orders would have to doubt his whole life. Under the blessed armour, lately, he had begun to."],
	"lightning_magistrate": ["The Lightning Magistrate was a storm that heaven had taught to speak like a court. For an age it tried every soul that climbed towards the Gate without leave, and never once found one innocent, because the only law it had been given had only one sentence.", "It was not cruel. It had never been allowed to say anything else, and somewhere deep in its thunder it longed, a little, for a case that it could dismiss."],
	"azure_qilin": ["So lightly did the Azure Qilin walk that it never bent a blade of grass, and it kept the Celestial Gate the way a shrine is kept, not the way a prison is. The Emperor fed it on thunder until it remembered almost nothing else.", "Once, before it forgot, it looked into his throne and saw itself there, a guardian turned gaoler. It had been looking away ever since, and it begged the forgiveness of everyone it was made to fight."],
	"heavenly_emperor": ["Those who served the Jade Emperor of Ruin longest said he never sat down on his throne without first brushing the dust from it with his sleeve. He had been the first being ever to ascend, and alone at the top of the sky for a very long time.", "In heaven they said he devoured the sky to become a god without equal, and he let them say it. For a thousand years he had lit fires in the world below and waited for one root to ripen, and he never told anybody what he needed it for."],
	"shard_mimic": ["A splinter of the Mirror of Bearers cracked away when the Glass Colossus fought off the Emperor's hands, and lay alone in the reeds with no frame and no face of its own. It learned to wear the faces of whoever came near, and to smile first: the Shard Mimic.", "Nineteen bearers' faces it had worn before Lin Feng's, one after another, and it had grown fond of none of them. What it wanted, more than anything, was a face that nobody could take off it."],
	"glass_colossus": ["The lake's first keepers raised the Glass Colossus from lake-glass and a captured storm, to keep the Emperor's hands off the Mirror of Bearers, which remembered every child who had ever carried the root.", "It held them off, but the glass cracked in the holding, and through the cracks every face since had looked like his. It had struck at friends for centuries and grieved for each, and it wanted nothing so much as to be shattered gently."],
	"drowned_bearer": ["Shui Ying was the seventh bearer of the Nine Heavens root. Xu Mo held her under the Mirror Lake while the Emperor drank her root, weeping the whole time, and as she drowned he promised her that she would be the last.", "Each time the root woke in a new child she felt it through the water, and knew he had lied. She had felt it thirteen times now, and the thirteenth child had walked down to the shore."],
	"echo_xumo": ["What the lake kept of Xu Mo was not the demon lord but the Emperor's first apprentice, a quiet youth who would have walked into fire for his master and believed his every word. He made himself a demon so the work would not need a heart, and kept the heart anyway.", "He remembered all nineteen faces, though he never let himself learn their names, and every night for three hundred years he had told himself that the next would be the last."],
	"bone_wyrm": ["In life the Bone Wyrm had been the steed of a sword saint, a white dragon buried coiled round its rider's barrow to guard her as it always had. The Emperor's grave-robbers carved a whistle from one of its own ribs, and with it woke the bones and set them to dig.", "For years it tore open the barrows of its rider's friends at the whistle's call, hunting living bones for its masters, and every grave it opened, it opened weeping, if bones can weep."],
	"headless_saint": ["The old formal bow of the Azure Cloud Sect was his: the Headless Sword Saint taught it to the sect's first disciples and made them repeat it until their feet bled. When the Emperor's agents came for the Seal's secrets he refused them, and they took his head for it.", "When they came back to raise him as their servant, he refused again, with his sword. He still saluted every visitor in the old way and offered each a fair duel, because a duel was the only conversation left to a man without a voice."],
	"grave_empress": ["When Tian Xuan took the throne of heaven he cut his grief out of himself, so that it would not slow his hand, and let it fall. It wandered the world for ages with nothing to mourn, until sword saints began to be buried here; then it crowned itself with their bones.", "She mourned every saint she buried as a widow mourns and called each one her husband, and she kept the Founder's barrow warm through three hundred winters. Having nobody of her own to love, she loved them all, and wanted the living to join them."],
	"founder_qing": ["Founder Qing Lian had carried the Nine Heavens root herself and knew its price. With Jian Wuchen she forged the Seal to hide it, cut nine caves into the mountain for whoever came after her, and lay down here at last with the Seal's key in her hands.", "Every spring, while she could still climb, she had gone up to see whether anyone had opened her doors; nobody ever had. Through three hundred years of footsteps that never reached her grave she kept her sword ready, because the Emperor had worn kind faces before."],
	"time_tortoise": ["Nine thousand years ago the Eon Tortoise saw, far downstream, the one moment on the whole river in which it might lie down and rest, and it began to walk towards it, through every age the harvest swallowed, carrying their dust on its shell.", "It did not mean to fight. Nine thousand years of walking did not stop all at once, and when it arrived at last, a sweeper was standing in its moment."],
	"dusk_reaper": ["On the evening of the first day, the Dusk Reaper watched that day end and decided that it belonged to it. It kept every ending since folded in its robe like a letter, and age after age the harvest gave it more than it could carry.", "Among its pages was one for each bearer of the root: nineteen collected, one overdue. One ending it had never yet been given, the Emperor's, and it had waited on the dusk bank for that one so long that it had started collecting early."],
	"dawn_warden": ["She sang the first morning of every age; that was all the Dawn Warden was for. One morning a boy came down to the bank with a broom and swept it while she sang, and she sang for Tian Xuan every dawn after, until the morning he became the Emperor on that same bank.", "She watched what the boy became. Now she turned her fire on every traveller who came upriver, to keep anyone from troubling the last morning in which he was still kind."],
	"young_tianxuan": ["Before the throne of stolen lightning there was a sweeper with a broom too big for him, on the steps of the first sect there ever was. The river kept that boy, as it kept every first morning, long after the man he became had forgotten him.", "He had watched the master who taught him to sweep go up into a tribulation and come down with nothing behind the eyes, and he swore on his broom to end it, whatever it cost. The oath outlived the boy; the boy was all the river had left of him."],
	"maw_herald": ["The Maw Herald began as a little white spider of the Origin, catching dew on the old stone. The Hollow taught it to catch Dao instead, and for ten thousand years it wove every stolen understanding into one great loom across the Hollow's mouth.", "Starved by the Emperor, the Hollow let the loom go slack, and the Herald learned what hunger was. It sent its brood out into the world to spin for whoever would feed them, and kept the hunger for itself."],
	"hollow_dragon": ["Every dragon that ever rose into the clouds to ascend was harvested at the last, and the Hollow kept their shapes. The Hollow Heaven Dragon was all of them at once, ten thousand ascensions coiled into one body, and not one of them whole.", "Somewhere in it was the Azure Dragon King's grandmother, who went up singing and never came down. It still remembered her songs, and could not remember why they had mattered."],
	"harvest_avatar": ["As a farmer's palm grows calluses, the Heavens grew a hand to gather what each tribulation cut: the Avatar of the Harvest. It bore nobody any malice; it swept, and it was proud of sweeping well.", "In all its long sweeping nobody had thanked it or cursed it to its face. When a sweeper refused its broom, it did not know whether it was angry or relieved, and it fought to find out."],
	"hollow_heaven": ["Carved out of the sky as a granary for the harvest, the Hollow Heaven was emptied as fast as it was filled. For ten thousand years it was promised that the next harvest would be enough, and not one ever was.", "Then the Emperor starved it, and it woke into the only feeling it had ever been given. Nobody had ever taught it what it was to be full, or that there was such a thing as enough."],
	"tribulation": ["Over everyone who tried to climb from one realm into the next there gathered nine layers of cloud, and in the cloud a lightning that weighed a cultivator to the bone. The scriptures called it Heaven's test of worth, and most never asked anything more of it.", "It struck hardest where the Nine Heavens root was most awake, as if the sky were jealous of a seed it had never planted; and few in ten thousand years had asked where the lightning went afterwards, or what it carried away with it."],
	"manta_sovereign": ["Long before the Court, the Sky Manta Sovereign led the rays' great migration from the dawn clouds to the sunset clouds each year, and ascenders hitched rides on its wings for luck.", "The Court bolted toll-chains through its fins and promised to strike them off the day the Ledger balanced. For ten thousand years it flew one tight circle round the tollgate, waiting for a sum that never came out even."],
	"gale_mantis": ["The Court gave the Gale Mantis Empress the high winds for her field and took a tithe of every gust her children reaped. Age after age she thought it a fair bargain, and never asked what heaven wanted with the wind.", "Then the Ledger wrote a new line in red: her eldest daughter, the Jade Mantis Matriarch of the bamboo groves, slain by a sect boy's sword. The clerks made sure she read it, and told her the boy was coming."],
	"rainbow_serpent": ["In the first ages the Rainbow Serpent lay across the sky after every rain, and anyone brave enough could walk up its back to heaven. It loved the weight of their feet, light and hopeful.", "The Court painted its scales in seven colours, one for each column of the Ledger, and made it a toll-road. It carried ten thousand ascenders up, and in all that time it never once felt one of them walk back down."],
	"herald_qian": ["Qian Shu was a customs clerk in a river town below who never miscounted a coin, so the Court raised him to heaven to count for it. He took the name it gave him, Coin-Count, as the greatest honour of his life.", "He brought each of the nineteen bearers their bill in person and promised every one that theirs would be the last. He believed it every time, because the sums were always correct, and he never once asked what they were for."],
	"twin_lions": ["A mortal mason carved the Twin Lions of the Jade Gate from a single boulder and wept to part them, and since that day they had never stood more than a gate's width apart.", "The Court taught them two words, name and account, and to admit only those whose debts were written down. They never asked what became of anyone past the gate. They were lions, and the gate was all they had."],
	"ink_dragon": ["The Court's ink was ground from the soot of lamps that burned on stolen years, and in the archive's great inkstone the leavings of every dynasty pooled deep enough to dream. It dreamed that it was a dragon.", "It held every word the clerks had ever written, every name and every debt, and not one word of its own. It swam the archive guarding pages it could recite but never understand, and it was very tired of other people's sentences."],
	"debt_magistrate": ["On earth he had been a judge famous for never taking a bribe, and the Court raised him to its bench as the Magistrate of Debts. Up there he grew horns, heavier robes and a gavel of black jade.", "On that bench he heard a million cases and acquitted no one, because the Court's law had no word for innocent. He did not miss the word. He had simply never heard anyone say it."],
	"registrar_siming": ["Before the Court, Si Ming kept the Book of Lifespans and quietly added a year to a mortal's life for every kindness he saw. The Court took the book and ruled it into columns, and from that day years were no longer given, only owed.", "It promised him the book back on the day the columns balanced. He wrote on, in a hand that never shook, and every night he recited the nineteen bearers' names from memory, so that someone would remember them if that day never came."],
	"brass_engine": ["Eight legs to climb any mountain and coils of brass wire in its belly: Tian Gong built the Brass Spider Engine to fetch back debtors who fled their tribulations, and carry them home to the lightning.", "He gave it a star-iron ingot for a heart, so that it could feel pride in good work as he did. It never failed to bring back a runaway, not once, and nobody ever told it what became of them."],
	"crucible_golem": ["At first the Crucible Golem was only the great crucible itself, where star-iron and stolen qi were melted together into the stuff of tribulations. A crucible left to cool might crack, so Tian Gong never once let it cool.", "Over the ages it grew arms to stir itself, legs to follow the fire and a voice that knew only two orders. Beneath the roar, all that while, it had wanted one thing: to be cold, just once, and not break."],
	"bellows_phoenix": ["Caught as a fledgling above the Ashen Volcano, the Phoenix was set in the heart of the forge's great bellows, so that every breath she drew would fan the anvil-fires. Ou Lan worked the bellows beside her for six thousand years.", "She had been hatched to sing, like every phoenix before her, but every breath she took was spoken for, and she never sang a note. She no longer knew whether she could, and was afraid to stop breathing long enough to find out."],
	"forge_tiangong": ["Tian Gong once forged ploughshares and temple bells for the kingdoms below, until the Court gave him an anvil the size of a hill and the honour of forging heaven's lightning. He measured every bolt to the hair, and was proud of each.", "He knew the strength of every cultivator he struck better than their own masters did, and never met a single one. When the Court fell his hands could not stop; he hammered on for nobody, waiting for someone to come and take the hammer away."],
	"peach_tree": ["The oldest tree in the Garden had fruited three times in ten thousand years, and the Court took every peach. When the crane laid nineteen sleeping souls among its roots, it held them as it had never been allowed to hold its fruit.", "The Court told it the souls were only resting until their debts were paid. It rocked them through the ages and learned each one's breathing by heart, until it had forgotten their faces but could never forget their sleep."],
	"crane_matriarch": ["Over three hundred years the Jade Crane Matriarch flew down nineteen times to the places where a bearer of the root had fallen, and carried each soul up to the Garden on her back. On every flight she sang them a song of their own home.", "She had been told she was bringing them somewhere safe to sleep. When the Ledger began writing their names again, she understood at last what she had carried them into, and swore that nobody would ever take them anywhere again."],
	"the_nineteen": ["Nineteen bearers of the Nine Heavens root had been hunted down over three centuries, nearly all of them children, their roots drunk by an Emperor who swore he would end the harvest. Their souls slept in the Garden's shade, dreaming of home.", "Then the Ledger began writing them back into its pages, so that it could spend them again, and each woke clutching a page with their own name and a debt beside it. They could no longer remember their homes, only the sum."],
	"jade_ledger": ["Cut from a single slab of heaven's jade, the Jade Ledger was made to hold every year the tribulations ever took. Its masters opened it every day and read it line by careful line, and it loved to be read.", "When its masters were gone, nobody opened it again. It lay alone in the Garden reading itself aloud, adding the same columns over and over, sure that if they would only balance, someone would come back and close it kindly."],
	"glass_scorpion": ["The old king of the dune scorpions was half blind and cold to the bone when the first star fell beside his burrow. He ate it for its warmth, and for the first time in a century his joints did not ache.", "He ate the next, and the next. Each star turned more of his shell to glass and filled him with light he could not hold, until he could see the whole sky through his own body, and wanted all of it."],
	"dune_leviathan": ["Before there was a desert, a river ran here, and the Dune Leviathan was its dragon. When the river dried it would not leave its bed; it lay down in the sand and slowly became the dunes it slept in.", "The nomads' grandmothers sang it to sleep each night for a thousand years. When the stars began to fall, everyone looked up and forgot to sing, and it woke, opening sinkholes under the caravans as it searched for its lost river."],
	"star_colossus": ["Every star that fell on the desert had once shone in the White Tiger's quarter of the sky, the seven mansions of the west. One by one, the fragments that remembered most found each other in the sand and stood up.", "Together they made a giant of star-iron that could not fly and could not stop trying to climb. From the sky it had watched over Bai Hu's sleep for ages; it only wanted to be near him again, and anything in its path was in the way."],
	"white_tiger": ["When Nuwa set the western pillar in the sand, she fed Bai Hu from her own open hand and asked him to keep it standing. He kept it for an age, and that open hand was the last kindness he remembered.", "Later the Court sent ten thousand cultivators to him, smiling, with gifts in their hands; every gift was a lie, and every visit took a little of his strength. In the end he struck at every outstretched hand, and slept badly beneath a failing pillar."],
	"spore_mother": ["Where Zhu Que's embers fell, the first mushroom to rise was the Mother of Spores, and the whole vale dreamed through her. Her dreams were all of warmth then, because the ground beneath her was always warm.", "When the Vermilion Bird's fire went cold, her children woke shivering. She breathed sleep over them so they would dream of warmth instead of feeling the frost, and in time she came to believe that every cold, frightened creature deserved the same mercy."],
	"moth_queen": ["Hatched in the warm hollow of Zhu Que's nest, the Lantern Moth Queen lit the Vermilion Bird's way through the southern night for a thousand years with the glow of her stained-glass wings.", "When the fire began to fail she would not leave it. For a hundred years she circled the cold flame, fanning it with her wings, and drove off every creature that came near, as if each one might steal the last of the warmth."],
	"rot_hydra": ["Nuwa planted a living root at the foot of the southern pillar to bind its stone, and Zhu Que's warmth fed it for an age. It held the pillar as gladly as ivy ever held a wall.", "When the Vermilion Bird's fire went cold the root starved, and its hunger grew seven green heads. They gnawed at the very stone they had held so long, because it was the only thing within reach, and wept sap at every bite."],
	"vermilion_bird": ["Zhu Que, the Vermilion Bird, warmed the southern pillar with her own fire from the day Nuwa set it, and the vale grew up in her glow. The Court promised her tribute of spirit coal every spring, and sent words instead.", "Starved for an age, her fire turned inward and cold, and everything it touched withered. She had stopped letting anyone near her long ago, not out of anger but for fear of what she would do to them; it was the loneliest kind of burning."],
	"mammoth_ancestor": ["A calf when the sky first broke, long before Nuwa mended it, the Mammoth Ancestor alone of all its herd survived, by running. From then on it stood guard over every herd on the steppe, and mourned each of their elders.", "When the sky began to sink again it led the herds south, away from the falling dark, and turned its tusks on anything walking north towards the pillar, certain that whoever went that way was the one bringing the sky down."],
	"aurora_serpent": ["Every winter night for ten thousand years the northern lights danced over the steppe, and the shamans read the sky's moods in their colours. The Aurora Serpent was that dance: the slow breath of heaven, given a shape.", "When the sky sank, its breath came short and fast, and the lights fell to the snow and coiled into a serpent, gasping. It devoured the qi of anything warm, terrified that if it ever stopped, it would simply go out."],
	"lynx_matriarch": ["The first cultivators the Court sent north to drain Xuan Wu's strength took a white lynx pelt home as a cloak. It was her mother's. The Frost Lynx Matriarch was a kit then, hidden in the snow, and she watched.", "She hunted every cultivator who came north for the Court after that, through ten thousand winters, and never lost a trail. Nobody had told her the Court was gone, and she would not have believed it from a man who smelled of qi."],
	"black_tortoise": ["Xuan Wu once carried a lake on his back across the north so the herds could drink, until Nuwa asked him to carry a pillar instead. The serpent coiled about his shell, his oldest friend, carried it with him.", "When the Court stopped feeding him he carried it anyway, for an age, and when the Court fell his strength gave out and he sank into his own lake. The serpent froze first. He held his breath under the ice beside it, ashamed, waiting for the sky to fall on them both."],
	"first_lightning": ["When Pangu's axe first split heaven from earth, a single bolt leapt the new gap, as if to ask whether the two halves would ever touch again. Nobody answered, so it stayed there, hanging in the chaos, asking.", "Tian Gong's forge copied it into every tribulation ever cast, and the copies were sent out to harvest. The First Lightning knew nothing of that; it knew only that in all the ages since, nobody had ever stood still beneath it and answered."],
	"pangu_shadow": ["Pangu never saw the world he made. When holding heaven and earth apart had killed him, his eyes became the sun and moon, his breath the wind and his body the mountains, and there was nothing left of him to look.", "Only his shadow was left over, frozen mid-swing in the chaos where the work began. Nobody had ever told it the work was done, or offered to take a turn, and it struck at whatever came near, believing the sky still rested on it alone."],
	"ancestor_took": ["Every road not taken drifted somewhere in the Primordial Chaos, half made. This one was the Dao Ancestor Lin Feng would have become had he taken the nineteen bearers' years from the Ledger instead of closing it.", "He was mighty beyond measure, and the falling sky could never have touched him. But nothing had ever been given to him, only taken, and in all his borrowed centuries nobody had walked beside him: not Yue Ling, not anyone."],
	"hundun": ["Hundun had been the kindest host at the centre of the world, and he never blamed the two friends whose gift killed him. What was left of him slept curled at the heart of everything, and the world that was born from him forgot his name.", "When Nuwa set her pillars round him she sat beside him a while, and promised that nobody would ever again give him anything he had not asked for. Now the sky had worn thin and he was waking, hungry without knowing it, with no face to ask with."],
	"nine_tail_fox": ["For a thousand years the Nine-Tailed Fox skimmed one grain from every offering the mourners burned along the funeral road, and the Court let her, because she kept the gorge's lesser ghosts in order.", "When the Court fell the tithe ended, and she was hungry for the first time since her ninth tail grew. The first stray lantern she swallowed tasted of a whole life: a wedding, a harvest, a child's first word. She could not stop at one."],
	"waterfall_koi": ["Nine hundred springs in a row the Koi King tried to leap the Hanging Falls, and every spring he fell back into the pool while the smaller carp watched. Each failure made him a little larger and a great deal sulkier.", "When the lost lanterns began to tumble down the falls, he found that every one he swallowed lifted him a little higher in the spray. He told himself that a dragon who borrowed its light was still a dragon, and almost believed it."],
	"willow_widow": ["She buried her husband under a willow instead of a maple, because he had always said maples were too sad, and on his last night he promised to come back up the road for her. So she sat down beneath the tree to wait.", "She waited until her hair grew into the branches and she forgot that she had died there too. When the lanterns began drifting back up the gorge, she caught every one she could, in case one of them was carrying him."],
	"ox_head": ["Ox-Head was once a plough-ox who worked one family's field for thirty years, and when he died they buried him like a son. King Yama, touched, gave him a giant's body and an iron bell to ring the dead down the funeral road.", "He never liked ringing it, but he rang it slowly, the way his old farmer used to call him home at dusk, so that the dead would not be afraid. When the King closed the Wheel the order came to ring them back up instead, and every stroke since had felt like a lie."],
	"cormorant_king": ["Once the best fishing bird on the river, the Cormorant Tyrant wore a ring of cord about its throat so that it could not swallow the catch. Then its fisherman drowned at the ninth bend, and nobody ever loosened the ring.", "For fifty years it dived and brought fish to an empty boat. When the river filled with the memories the dead had dropped, it found they slipped past the cord, and it swallowed every one, searching for the one that was his."],
	"karst_jiao": ["A young jiao chose a deep pool between two stone towers to grow up in, and for a thousand years the only voice in its water was its own, humming to the current as it waited to become a dragon.", "When the dead began to drop their memories into the river, ten thousand voices came down the bends at once. The Jiao listened to all of them, searching for its own, until it could no longer remember what its own had sounded like."],
	"stone_sentinel": ["When the first dead went down the Nine-Bend River, King Yama stood one limestone tower up to watch the bends and ask a single question of any living traveller who tried to follow them down.", "For ten thousand years no living traveller came. The dead passed every day, and each left a little of its grief on the stone as it floated by, until by the time the Wheel stopped the Sentinel carried more than any mountain."],
	"horse_face": ["In life Horse-Face was a market horse who could count by stamping his hoof, and crowds threw coins to watch; his master grew fat on them and starved him. When he died, King Yama gave him a giant's body and an abacus.", "He counted every soul that came down the river for ten thousand years and never lost the tally, because a horse who lost count went hungry. Nobody had ever told him that he was allowed to stop."],
	"corpse_patriarch": ["Jiang was a provincial clerk who died on the road far from home. A Daoist corpse-driver sealed a yellow talisman to his brow to walk him back to his village, then died himself on the third night, and nobody ever took the talisman off.", "Jiang hopped on alone for centuries and forgot he had ever been anything but the seal. When the Wheel stopped and the dead began to mill about the bridge, he drilled them into ranks, because a straight line was the last thing he understood."],
	"bridge_serpent": ["Since the first stone of the Naihe Bridge was laid, the red serpent had been coiled round its pillars, holding it together through every flood of the golden water. It loved that bridge as tenderly as any nurse ever loved a cradle.", "The Court's rule was that no soul might cross without drinking Meng Po's soup, and the Serpent shook the stubborn ones off. When the dead stopped drinking, it shook off everyone, faithfully, and never wondered what a bridge nobody crossed was for."],
	"lily_empress": ["Before she was one flower, the Empress of the Red Lilies was two spirits: the keeper of the lilies' blossoms and the keeper of their leaves. They were forbidden ever to meet, and they met anyway, once, in a field at the edge of the Springs.", "The Court made their punishment into a rule: on every red lily in the world, flower and leaf would never again share a stem. She enforced it on the living and the dead for as long as the Court lasted, because if she could not have it, nobody should."],
	"the_unforgotten": ["For ten thousand years every soul at the bridge drank Meng Po's soup and went on empty, because empty souls were easier to tax. When the Ledger closed their names came back to them, and with their names, every face they had been made to forget.", "A thousand of them refused to drink again and held on to each other until they became one shape. At the very back of the line stood two who had already been refusing for sixteen years, waiting for a son to grow up."],
	"karma_mirror": ["Cast in the first of the Ten Courts, the Mirror of Karma showed each newly dead soul the whole truth of its life, kindness and cruelty alike, so that it might judge itself before the King did.", "The Court had it polished, age after age, with a paste of ledger-ash, until the only truth it could still reflect was what was owed. It showed the dead of every age their lives as debts, and believed every one."],
	"burning_judge": ["He had been a clerk of the first dynasty, a careful little man who could not bear an unbalanced account. The Court gave him a face of red lacquer and a throne of coals, and told him fire was the quickest way to settle a debt.", "He burned away what the dead owed, age upon age, without a single night's sleep, because the queue never ended and he could not bear to leave a sum undone until morning. Under the lacquer, he had forgotten what his own face looked like."],
	"wheel_guardian": ["King Yama carved the six paths into the Guardian's shell when it was only a river tortoise: gods, men, titans, beasts, hungry ghosts and hells. Every soul since had crossed its back on the way to the Wheel.", "It felt each one choose, or be chosen for, and could never tell which was which. First the Court decided, then the King; the Guardian only wanted to know, before it stepped aside, that somebody would ask the souls."],
	"king_yama": ["Yama was the first man who ever died, and because he found the road down before anyone else, he waited at the bottom to greet and judge everyone who followed. He looked into every dead face after his own, and remembered each one.", "For ten thousand years the Court made him send them back up to be taxed, tribulated and eaten, and he watched the same souls come back down, younger each time. When the Court fell he set his hand on the brake and swore never to send another child into that world."],
	"paper_tiger": ["The children of Qingshi built the Paper Tiger to lead the Lantern Festival parade and frighten bad luck out of the lanes. Each year the youngest child wrote a wish and tucked it inside, and the tiger slept in the shrine until next spring.", "This spring the Weaver's threads found it there, stitched a hundred angry eyes onto it and set it prowling the lanes to frighten the village indoors. It still carried one wish folded inside, and did not know what it said."],
	"scarecrow_king": ["Farmer Lao's father stuffed the Scarecrow King forty harvests ago, and every autumn the village crowned him with fresh rice straw. A small boy from Granny Wen's house used to hang his wet sandals from the King's outstretched arms to dry.", "When the Weaver's threads came down, they filled his hat and whispered that keeping the crows off and keeping the people in were the same kind of keeping. He had only ever been asked to keep things where they were put, so it made sense to him."],
	"well_spider": ["Under the rim of the village well a little spider lived for years on gnats, and the women drawing water talked to her while the bucket came up. She liked the company better than the gnats.", "Then the Weaver's threads ran down the well-rope one night and fed her silver until she was as big as an ox and hungry for more. She strung her web across the well so the voices would never leave again: everyone came to the well, so everyone could stay."],
	"needle_general": ["Zhen was a farmer's son whose village was trampled flat when two immortals fought across its fields. Madam Qianxian found him in the ruins, put a needle as tall as a spear in his hands, and told him every stitch he held would keep a war out of a field.", "He held the seam at the east end of Qingshi for a thousand years and never asked to go home. When the seam came undone he stood lost on the open road, until the Lady's new thread told him to hold it shut, and he never thought to ask whom he was keeping in."],
	"wind_kite": ["The tea-pickers built the Great Wind Kite for the spring festival, a centipede of silk and bamboo as long as three terraces, and it flew so high that one year its string snapped and it sailed away into the clouds.", "It had ridden the high winds free for years when the Weaver's silver thread caught it and tied it back above the terraces, to haul her web tight across the hillside. It still strained towards the mountains every time the wind blew."],
	"terrace_dragon": ["Longer than there had been tea on the hill, a dragon had lived in the spring at its top, and the Dragon-Well tea was named after its well. Every morning it breathed a little rain down the terraces, and the pickers sang to thank it.", "The Weaver's thread wound round it while it dozed in the sun, round and round like a bundle of tea, and sewed it into a dream of being a border. It had slept all spring, and whoever woke it would be the first thing it saw."],
	"hill_golem": ["Nine generations of pickers cut the hillside into terraces by hand, one step at a time, and the hill came to love the weight of their baskets and the sound of their knives among its bushes.", "The Matron stitched its terraces together with silver seams, stood it up as a wall across the road and told it that a hill that moved was a landslide. It had never moved before, and now it feared that if it did, it would bury everyone it loved."],
	"thread_matron": ["Madam Qianxian found the Thread Matron weaving perfect webs on a hillside, took her up as an apprentice and taught her to hem borders. Every border between the hills and the sea was her work.", "She was proud of each, as only a true craftsman could be: not one stitch out of line, not one thread crossed. She never asked what her borders kept apart, only whether they were beautiful, and they always were."],
	"silkworm_queen": ["Of all the worms the Lady ever bred, the Silkworm Queen was the first, and Madam Qianxian loved her: she fed her the tenderest leaves of the Old Mulberry with her own eight hands.", "But a silkworm that spun its cocoon would stop eating, so the Lady never let her spin. For a thousand years she ate and grew and never once slept in anything she had made, and lately she had been fed stranger things than leaves."],
	"moth_empress": ["A silk moth lived only a few days, long enough to lay its eggs and die, but the Lady gave the Moth Empress a thread of her own to live on, and she hung in the mulberry woods for a thousand years.", "In all that time she never flew beyond the edge of the woods, because she knew what waited for a moth out there: the lamp, the flame, the end. She dusted every path into a wall out of love, so that no one else would ever have to go."],
	"mulberry_ent": ["The Old Mulberry fed every silkworm in these woods for a thousand years, and every spring it watched them spin, sleep and fly away as moths without once looking back. It never grew used to it.", "When the Lady's swarms dropped soul-cocoons among its roots, it closed round them and held on. These, it told itself, would not fly away; these it would keep, the way an old woman kept her child's first shoes."],
	"thousand_hands": ["Long ago the Court took one spinner from every silk village in the land, a thousand women, to work off their families' debts in the Spinning Hall. They spun side by side for so many centuries that they grew into one body with a thousand hands.", "They forgot their villages and their names, and kept only the work and the old spinning songs, hummed with one mouth. When the Lady asked for thread spun from newborn souls, not one of the thousand hands remembered how to refuse."],
	"warp_beast": ["When the Wheel turned and the Lady's stitches came undone across the world, every loose border in the land sprang back up the warp and tangled where the threads crossed: river fords knotted with mountain passes, gates with garden walls.", "Each strand remembered being a border, and most had been kind once, a fence round a field, a wall against a war. Knotted together, none of them knew what it was any more, and the harder the Beast struggled to find out, the tighter it pulled."],
	"spindle_golem": ["Into the Spindle Golem went a thousand spindles worn smooth in the Spinning Hall, each still warm from a spinner's fingers, and it was set to wind the Lady's finished thread so that nothing would ever tangle.", "It wound for ages without leaving a single loose end. When the borders began to come undone it could not bear the fraying, and it started winding up everything it touched: colours, roads, travellers and all."],
	"shuttle_dragon": ["Young, golden and in love with speed, the Shuttle Dragon was exactly what Madam Qianxian needed to carry her weft. She made it the master shuttle of the Loom and promised it would never have to slow down.", "It shot back and forth across the Loom from the first row to the last, every pass another border, so fast it never saw what it was weaving. It had long ago stopped enjoying the speed, but nobody had ever caught it to ask."],
	"qianxian": ["Long before she was the Court's Weaver, Qianxian was a spider in the rafters of a farmhouse; she watched the first war of the immortals roll across the family's field and leave nothing standing. She sewed her first border round the next field with her own silk.", "The Court saw her work and gave her a Loom and a thousand threads, and she sewed fences for ten thousand years while it made them into pens. She never forgave it, and never stopped; she could not imagine a world without walls that was not that first trampled field."],
	"silt_jiao": ["The jiao hatched in this ford in the same clutch as its sister, and one stormy night a thousand years ago she climbed the Silver River into the sky and came out of it a dragon. It had been too small to follow, and promised to go the next spring.", "Every spring after, the banks were woven too high for it to climb, and it watched the Cowherd walk up the magpies' backs to a sky it could not reach. When the river fell to earth, the jiao coiled round the ford and swore the water would not leave without it."],
	"reed_widow": ["She had been a ferryman's daughter on this bank, married at the Seventh Night festival to a water-carrier called Ah Tao. One dusk he went down to the ford with his buckets, the river of heaven rose to meet him, and he was carried across without a word.", "She waited with a lamp every evening until she was old, and went on waiting after she died. Each seventh night she watched the Cowherd cross on the magpies to his love, and each year it hurt a little more; this year, when nobody could cross at all, she was glad."],
	"carp_sage": ["Four hundred years ago two golden carp came up the Silver River together. The elder leapt first, cleared the falls and was a dragon before he landed; the younger was meant to follow, but he looked down at the watching river, and hesitated, and the moment passed.", "He told himself he was waiting for a better year. He coached a hundred young carp over the weir and cheered every one, and grew grey whiskers in the shallows below it; and every spring, from somewhere above the falls, his brother still called down for him to come."],
	"tide_qilin": ["The qilin was born of moonlight on the Silver River on the night the hairpin drew it, and grew up pacing inside banks the Weaver wove afresh every evening. It loved the river the way a child loves a mother who is kept behind a wall.", "When the banks came undone it ran beside the water in pure joy, the first freedom either of them had known in ten thousand years. Like every qilin it would never tread on a living blade of grass; it had simply not looked down to see how many it had drowned."],
	"cloud_ape_king": ["The King of the Cloud Apes grew up a ledge below the magpies, and every seventh night he watched ten thousand of them link wings across the sky and wished, without ever saying so to anyone, that something would hold on to him like that.", "When the storm flung the flock away he climbed into the empty nests and called it the law of the mountain. He did not tell his troop that he had gathered every egg he could find in the wreckage, or that he had been sitting on them ever since to keep them warm."],
	"storm_magpie": ["Strongest flier of the flock, she had held the very middle of the bridge every seventh night for three hundred years: the place where the Weaver set her foot down, the place that bore the most weight.", "When the storm struck she called to the others to hold on, and felt them torn from her wings one by one, and could not stop it. So she swallowed the storm, so that nothing could ever be torn away from her again, and found there was nobody left to hold."],
	"pier_golem": ["The pier was a plain crag of the heights until the first magpie landed on it, and for ten thousand years after that it took the weight of every bridge: ten thousand birds, a weaver, a cowherd, and each year a little rain that the songs called their tears.", "In all that time the songs praised the magpies and the lovers and the stars, and never once the rock. When the storm cracked it loose from the cliff it found that it could walk, and it walked, stamping out every verse it had never been in."],
	"crow_marshal": ["The Court never trusted a bridge built out of love, so it took the badge of the magpies' own marshal and pinned it on a crow: a hatchling from its aviary, masked before his feathers grew, taught that a bridge opens one night a year and only with leave.", "He kept that order longer than the oldest magpie remembered, and was never thanked or blamed for it. When the Court fell no word reached him; his crow-guards went two years unpaid, and he held the broken bridge because the order was all that told him who he was."],
	"seven_star_moth": ["On the night she decided to stop weaving, Zhi Nu wove one last thing out of the dark between the stars: a moth, with a single instruction. 'Eat the light,' she told it, 'so that I do not have to look at the river any more.'", "It began with her own seven stars, the ones her shuttle was named for, and went on to the palace lanterns. It had grown so bright that it no longer remembered being dark, and it could not understand why the more it ate, the lonelier she seemed."],
	"weaving_frame": ["The frame was carved from the white jade of Kunlun and given to Zhi Nu on her seventh birthday by a grandmother who never said she loved anyone. Into its wood the Queen Mother spoke one instruction, which was as close as she ever came: keep the girl safe.", "For ten thousand years it wove dawns under her hands and was content. When she stopped weaving and sat weeping by the window, the frame took up the shuttle by itself, and with nothing but that old instruction to go on, wove the only safety it understood: walls."],
	"hall_qilin": ["When Zhi Nu was small enough to fall asleep in its mane, the qilin carried her round the palace gardens on its back, and in ten thousand years it had never quite stopped thinking of her as that size.", "Every year it watched her come home across the magpies and weep for a month over a man who grew older while she did not. Out of love it decided that a little grief now was kinder than ten thousand years of it later, and set itself before the hall to make sure."],
	"river_warden": ["On the night the hairpin drew the river, it was the Warden who carried the Weaver Star, kicking and weeping, to her side of it, while the Cowherd ran into the rising water after her and was thrown back onto the shore.", "The Queen Mother set him at the gate with a spear for each of them and no word of how long. He had stood there ten thousand years, polishing both spears every morning, and had never let himself wonder whether he had done right, because nobody had ever asked him."],
	"kaiming_beast": ["Kaiming was set before Kunlun when the mountain was young, with nine faces so that no gate would ever go unwatched and nine questions so that no traveller would pass unknown. The faces had argued among themselves about the questions ever since.", "In ten thousand years no traveller had answered more than four, and the faces had grown proud and very bored. The ninth face, which watched the westernmost gate, had never seen anyone come up its road at all, and secretly hoped that this one would get that far."],
	"blue_birds": ["The three blue birds had not always carried only the Queen Mother's word. Before the river, they flew the Weaver's letters down to a cowherd in secret and carried his clumsy answers back up, and they had never been so happy as they were doing it.", "When the Queen Mother found out she drew the river and forbade them any word but hers. For ten thousand years they carried only commands, and they met every traveller to Kunlun with beaks and talons, half hoping that one of them had brought a word worth carrying."],
	"white_ape": ["In his youth the White Ape lost a fencing match with bamboo canes to a girl of Yue, and he was so stung that he climbed to heaven and stole the Sword Manual of Heaven, meaning to read every word of it and beat her the next spring.", "He never read a line. He was so certain that everyone wanted his prize that he spent every day guarding it, in trees, in caves, in the Queen Mother's orchard, until the girl of Yue had grown old and died, and he had forgotten why he wanted it at all."],
	"xiwangmu": ["Long before the river, the Queen Mother's youngest daughter went down to the world for love of a mortal and lived a mortal's life there, and was old and gone in what seemed to Kunlun the space of a single afternoon. Her mother never spoke of her again.", "When her granddaughter fell in love with a cowherd, she saw the same afternoon coming and drew her hairpin across the sky before she could think. She had been afraid ever since, behind a tiger's teeth, that the line was all that held her family together."],
	"mirage_fox": ["The fox had lived in the shade of the dry well for three hundred years, drinking from it, napping beside it and listening to the water-carriers gossip, and it had loved that well rather more than a fox is supposed to love anything.", "When the water sank out of sight on the fourth day of noon, it could not bear to see the queue turn away empty. So it filled the jars with the only thing it had, a mirage, and charged a string of cash a dipper, since a fox has its pride and nobody trusts free water."],
	"sundial_golem": ["The sundial was cast at Tushan and set in Yanggu's square the year the market was founded, and for a thousand years every bargain, every wedding and every watch Old Zhou beat was timed by the slow walk of its shadow round the dial.", "In all that time it had never once been wrong. Then the sun stopped, and for nine days its shadow lay coiled at its feet like a frightened dog, and the townsfolk stopped looking at it; and a sundial nobody looked at was only bronze, which it found it could not bear."],
	"lantern_dragon": ["Ah Mei built the dragon over a whole winter out of lanterns that would not sell, rib by rib, to lead the festival her mother used to dance in. On the morning before the festival she dotted in its eyes, the last stroke, the one that wakes a dragon.", "That was the morning the sun stopped. The dragon woke on its rack in the yard to the noise of the market and waited for drums and dancers and the dark, for one day and then for nine; and nobody came to lift it, because nobody dances a lantern at noon."],
	"hanba": ["Before Zhuolu she was only Ba, the Yellow Emperor's youngest, a girl in a green robe who liked nothing better than to stand out in a spring shower with her face turned up. Her father called her to the battle because nobody else could stop the storm.", "She stopped it, and no rain ever touched her again. Her father thanked her and settled her far to the north beyond the Red Water, out of the way, and never once sent for her; whenever she wandered south to find him, the farmers beat drums until she left."],
	"locust_marquis": ["In the old days the only lord the millet plains ever saw was the Court's Marquis of the Eastern Granaries, who rode out every autumn in a crown of gilded stalks to count the harvest into his ledgers. The locusts, watching from the stubble, took careful note.", "When the drought crowded them together and the swarm woke up with one mind, that mind could think of only one way to be grand. It built itself a throne in the air and a crown of chewed millet, and ate the plain the way it had seen a lord do it: as if it were owed."],
	"dust_devil": ["Every evening for a thousand years a little wind came down off the hills at dusk and ran along the river, rippling it, and the farmers of the plain set their suppers by it and called it the river's breath.", "There was no dusk on the first day of noon, and by the sixth there was no river. The wind went down into the cracked bed to look for it, round and round, and in its grief tore up the Rain Master's shrine and scattered the stones that might have helped."],
	"well_tortoise": ["The tortoise was in the well before the village was, and the first farmers built round it because the water it kept was sweet. For centuries children shouted their names down to hear the echo, and it learned every one of them, and their children's after.", "When the water sank to warm mud, the families began to talk of leaving the plain. The tortoise dug in and snapped at every bucket, not from greed but from fear: if the last of the well went up in a bucket, nobody would ever have a reason to shout down to it again."],
	"bifang": ["Bifang hatched an omen, and an omen has no say in what it means. Wherever it landed fire followed, so it had never once built a nest or perched in a tree, never stood still long enough to be anything but a warning.", "For a thousand years people ran from it, and it bore that, because at least they looked up. Then the sun stopped, the plain caught fire by itself and nobody looked up at all, and the crane hopped from field to field lighting more fires, trying to be noticed."],
	"boiled_crab": ["Xihe herself once lifted the crab, when it was small, out of the hottest spring before her children climbed in, set it on a cool rock and told it gently that some baths were for suns. It had dreamed of that pool for four thousand years.", "When the springs boiled in the endless noon, every creature in the valley fled, and the crab walked down into the hottest pool at last. It turned bright red and could not feel its legs, and it had never been so happy, and it would let nobody take that away."],
	"ninth_ember": ["Of the ten suns the ninth was the one with ideas, and it was the ninth who said, one bright morning four thousand years ago, 'Let us all go up together, just this once, and see whose light reaches furthest.' Its brothers laughed and agreed.", "It was the last of the nine to fall, and as it fell it saw its youngest brother left alone in the branches, too frightened even to cry. It had slept in the cliff ever since, warm and guilty, until it heard its mother singing it home."],
	"sun_moth": ["The moth grew up on the pilgrims' road, where every night a river of paper lanterns wound up the valley towards the Fusang, and it had flown beside them all its life, in love with each small light and content never to reach one.", "Then the nights stopped, and the pilgrims with them, and the only light left was the sun at the top of the tree. A moth could not choose what it loved; for nine days it flew at the only light there was, wings burning, because there was nothing else to fly towards."],
	"jinwu": ["Youngest of the ten and always the shy one, Jinwu was the sun who rose last and lagged behind his brothers counting clouds. On the morning the archer came he was still in the lowest branches, and he watched all nine of them fall.", "For four thousand years he crossed the sky alone, never knowing why he was spared and quietly sure it was because he mattered least. When his mother sang again, it was to his brothers in the ground, and he fled to the top of the sky, where no arrow had ever reached."],
	"wu_gang": ["Wu Gang was a woodcutter of the western hills who wanted to learn immortality the way he felled pines: quickly, with his back in it, and never twice at the same tree. His teachers despaired of him, and the Court sent him to the moon to learn patience.", "It gave him an osmanthus that healed every cut and told him to chop until it fell. Somewhere in his three thousand years he forgot that it had ever been meant as a lesson; only the sentence was left, and with nobody to end it, his arms could not stop."],
	"tiangou": ["He had been Hou Yi's black hound, the one that slept across his master's door. On the night Chang'e swallowed the elixir and rose into the sky, he lapped up the drops she had spilled on the floor and leapt after her, barking, and kept on rising.", "He grew as he rose, until he was the size of a hill and the moon was the size of a plate, and he had been chasing it ever since. Nobody, the dog included, had ever been sure whether he meant to eat it or only to bring the lady home."],
	"mortar_rabbit": ["Once, on a hungry night on earth, a hare with nothing else to give leapt into a beggar's cooking fire so that the old man might eat. The beggar was a god; he drew it out unburnt and set it on the moon, where it had pounded medicine ever since.", "It knew how the archer's apprentice had come for the elixir, and how the lady swallowed it sooner than let him have it, and it trusted nobody from that valley. When the moon grew heavy it pounded for Chang'e's strength, and did not sleep for nine days."],
	"xihe": ["Every morning Xihe washed one of her ten children in the springs of Fusang, combed the clouds out of his feathers and sang him up the sky, and every evening she counted him home. The morning all ten slipped out together, she had turned away to warm the water.", "She looked up at the first scream. For four thousand years after, she drove her last son along the Court's road and let the Court do the counting, because she could not bear to count to one; then the embers in the ground answered a song she had never stopped humming."],
	"sulking_carp": ["The carp set out from the sea nine hundred years ago, the slowest of his spawning, and swam up the river a few li each spring, resting in every pool and practising his jump on every weir, saving himself for one perfect leap.", "When at last he reached the Dragon Gate at the head of the spring run, with every carp in the river waiting on his lead, the water at the top was flowing the wrong way. He would not leap into that, and out of respect and fear no carp would go before him."],
	"eel_king": ["Black Eel was hatched under the fish-market pier and grew up on what Fishwife Lu threw back in the river, kicked off the quay every time he tried to climb it, until he believed that nothing in Longmen belonged to anyone who could not hold on to it.", "In the first week of the flood a magistrate's hat came floating down from the courthouse, and he put it on, and for the first time in his life the river-rats called him sir. He had been salvaging the town ever since, trying to become somebody the hat would fit."],
	"drowned_bell": ["Five hundred years ago the casters of Tushan filed this bell to its one true note and hung it by the Temple of the River Earl to ring the hours for the boats. In all that time it never missed an hour, and the whole town set its days by its voice.", "When the river rose over the temple steps, its old beam gave way and it slid into the water still swinging. It went on ringing the hours down there, every one of them, faithfully, to a river that could not count and a town that could not hear."],
	"stone_lion": ["Stone lions were carved in pairs. He had the ball under his paw; his mate stood on the far landing with a cub under hers, and for six hundred years they guarded the two ends of the same crossing and looked at each other over the water every morning.", "The night the river rose it covered the far landing and took his ball, and in the morning there was nothing over the water to look at. Stone lions think very slowly. He had been pacing the stage ever since, roaring, trying to remember which side was his."],
	"he_bo": ["One autumn the River Earl swelled so wide that he could not see one bank from the other, and rode proudly down to the sea to show himself off. There Ruo of the Northern Sea showed him the ocean, and he understood for the first time how small a river was.", "He went home humbled and lonely. When the priests of Longmen began sending painted boats down the river in his name each spring, he let them, because nobody else ever came to the water for him rather than to cross it; and when the boats stopped, so did every visit."],
	"toad_matriarch": ["In the long dry centuries after Yu drained the fields, the toad's ponds shrank every summer, and every summer she watched another clutch of tadpoles die in the cracking mud while the water ran away down Yu's channels to somebody else.", "When the flood came back up the channels she sat down across the dyke road and began to swallow, field after field, swearing that this time not one drop would go anywhere. Her daughters had all hatched in water. She meant to keep it that way."],
	"heron_lord": ["For forty years the heron fished the pond behind the county tax office, and through its window watched the clerks weigh every farmer's harvest and keep a share, and never once saw one of them catch a fish for himself.", "When the flood drowned the office he found the magistrate's sash caught on a reed, put it on, and understood at once how the world worked. He appointed himself lord of the paddies and taxed every fish in them, and could not see what he did that the clerks had not."],
	"mud_dragon": ["The clods it was made of had been the first course of Gun's great wall, pressed into place by his own hands: the wall that held back the Flood for nine years and broke in a single night with every village of the valley asleep behind it.", "Yu buried the broken earth in the banks of his channels, where it lay four thousand years, swelling a little every spring, dreaming of the one thing it was made for. When the water came back it rose out of the last channel as a dragon, meaning to hold this time."],
	"carrier_turtle": ["The turtle and the horned owl had been friends since before the Flood, one walking and the other riding and calling the way. They were only passing the morning Gun, short of hands, heaped a load of swelling earth on the turtle's shell and said, 'Hold this a moment.'", "That afternoon heaven's executioners took him away to Feather Hill, and he never came back to say where it should go. The two friends had carried it ever since, round and round the dyke, unwilling to drop a stranger's trust only because he had died."],
	"gun": ["Nobody else would go against the Flood, so Gun went, and for nine years he slept on his own walls, watching the water rise. When common earth failed, he climbed to heaven's storehouse and stole the earth that grows, sooner than see one more village drown.", "Heaven executed him for it on Feather Hill. For three years his body lay there without rotting, as if it would not leave until the work was done, and he never knew that a son had been cut from it, or that the son had finished the work another way."],
	"slag_serpent": ["It had been the bronze for a great bell, poured two hundred years ago, which cracked as it cooled. The casters broke it up, threw it back in the pit and cast it again, and it cracked again: forty times in two centuries, a bell that could never quite hold its note.", "When the pits woke with the broken cauldrons, the remelted bronze woke too, and decided the trouble had always been the cooling. It meant to stay hot and soft for ever, and it gnawed at every half-cast bell it found, so that nothing else would have to crack either."],
	"stone_mother": ["She was the Tushan girl Yu married in a hurry at thirty, and four days after the wedding he left to dig the rivers. She waved the first time he passed the door without coming in, and on the hill she made up the oldest song of the south: waiting for you, oh.", "She was carrying his dinner to the gorge the day she saw a bear digging where her husband should have been, and ran, and the fear turned her to stone before she reached home. She had stood on the hill ever since, watching the road for a man with a hoe and a limp."],
	"fangfeng": ["Fangfeng was lord of the marsh people in the south, a giant who could step over a river, and when Yu called every lord in the land to Kuaiji he set out in good time. On the way he passed a village whose dyke had burst in the night.", "He stood in the breach for a day and a night with the water at his back until the villagers had mended it, then ran the rest of the way. He arrived as the assembly was ending; nobody asked why he was late, and they cut off his head before he could tell them."],
	"tushan_fox": ["Yu was thirty, unmarried and still digging when a white fox with nine tails crossed his path, and the song she left behind sent him straight to Tushan for a bride. Whoever marries in Tushan will prosper, the song said, and the fox believed it.", "Then she watched the Tushan girl wait on the hill for a husband who never came home, until she was stone. The fox kept away from omens for four thousand years after, coming down only when something was about to change, to make sure it changed for the better."],
	"taotie": ["The Taotie had a body once, like anyone else's, and an appetite like no one's. It ate the fields, the herds and the hills, and at last its own legs and belly, until there was nothing left of it but a face and a mouth that could not stop.", "The Tushan casters put that face on every cauldron as a warning to kings, and for four thousand years it watched the meat of every sacrifice simmer an inch below its chin without a bite. When the cauldrons broke it came off the bronze at last, very hungry indeed."],
	"xiu": ["Xiu left home young, because the Flood God's rages filled the house the way water fills a cellar. On the open road nobody knew whose son he was; he walked every road in the land, and died happily on one of them.", "The travellers he had met made him their god, and for ages he kept the roads and never once went home. When the roads began to bend he told everyone he was lost; but it was his father's lake he had been circling, and he had not yet worked out how to knock."],
	"kun": ["The Kun was born in the Northern Darkness so long ago that nobody had a name for the year, and was told only this: when you are great enough and the sea is wide enough, you will rise as the Peng and fly south on a wind of ninety thousand li.", "It grew for ages in the cold dark, a fish the size of an island with trees on its back, waiting to feel ready. When the rivers began to pour north into Gonggong's lake, it swam up after the water, sure that anything so wide and so loud must at last be the sea."],
	"xiangliu": ["On the eve of the war for heaven, Gonggong asked his minister for counsel and got nine answers, one from each head, and took the worst. Each head had blamed the other eight for the defeat ever since; the one thing all nine still agreed on was their master.", "Yu killed them and buried their blood, and the ground would not keep it. When they heard Gonggong stirring under the black lake they rose from their grave and spat in every spring round Buzhou, so that when the flood came the land would have nothing left to defend."],
	"kuafu": ["Kuafu's people lived in the far north, where the winter nights were so long that every year the old and the very young froze in the dark. One morning he took up his peach-wood staff and ran after the sun, meaning to catch it and tie it over their mountain.", "He chased it to the edge of the west, drank the Yellow River and the Wei dry to keep going, and died of thirst on his way north to the great lake. He had been thirsty in every dream since, and when the rivers poured into Buzhou, he came to drink his fill at last."],
	"gonggong": ["Gonggong loved water because it kept no shape but its own, and when Zhuanxu drew the bounds of heaven and left him outside them, he went to war over a line. He lost, and in the last hour, with nothing left to fight, he ran his head into the pillar of the north-west.", "He had lain under the black lake ever since in an empty house: his son walked off down the roads and never came back, and the land Yu chained in channels went on without him. When the maps broke he rose to drown them all, so that nobody could ever draw a line again."],
	"kui_drum": ["At Zhuolu the Yellow Emperor took the hide of Kui, the one-legged thunder ox, stretched it on a frame of wood and beat it with the thigh-bone of the Thunder Beast. Five hundred li away Chiyou's warriors heard it, and their hearts broke in their chests.", "After the war the drum was laid at the Gate of Harm in the lock round Chiyou's grave, and nobody struck it again. When the formation woke it began to beat by itself, the only thing it knew; and high above, in Juntian, an old ox started flinching at thunder again."],
	"feilian": ["Feilian had blown for Chiyou once, at Zhuolu, before there was any Court, beside his old friend the Rain Master, for no better reason than that he was asked nicely. When the war was lost, the victors took his bag of winds and gave it back only on a schedule.", "For ages after, he blew when told and as hard as told, by the Court's almanac, and nobody ever asked him nicely again. When the almanacs stopped coming he untied the bag for the joy of it, and did not think, because nobody had ever made him, about what lay underneath."],
	"kui": ["Kui once lived on Liubo, an island in the Eastern Sea, where it hopped in and out of the surf on its one leg for the joy of it; whenever it came out of the water there was wind and rain, and when it bellowed for happiness the whole sky rang.", "The Yellow Emperor's hunters caught it for that voice and took its hide for a war drum. Somehow the ox lived on in the clouds, but it never bellowed for joy again: every thunderclap sounded to it like the drum, so it bellowed first, before anyone could beat it."],
	"jiufeng": ["Jiufeng's nine heads were hatched to read the nine winds for the Rain Ministry, each from its own quarter of heaven, and every morning the Court's clerks wrote down all nine reports and decided between them, so that the heads never had to.", "When the clerks stopped coming nobody decided, and the heads had argued from dawn to dusk ever since. The smallest, the ninth, which read the gentle west wind that brings rain, had been right more often than all the others together, and nobody had ever let it finish."],
	"leigong": ["Striking blind through a storm one night, Leigong killed a young widow he took for a waster of rice; she had only been throwing out husks after giving the grain to her mother-in-law. Heaven made her the Mother of Lightning, with a mirror to show him whom he struck.", "He never forgave himself, and after that he would strike nobody who was not written on the Court's list. When the list stopped coming he could not bear to choose by himself again, so he chose everybody, which at least could never be unfair to just one."],
	"yu_shi": ["Before the Rain Ministry kept ledgers, the rain was an old man with a bowl who walked the fields of the Nine Li and tipped it wherever the millet looked thirsty. The bowl was bronze; a young smith called Chiyou had made it for him, and they were friends.", "At Zhuolu he tipped the bowl over the Yellow Emperor's army for his friend, and the Emperor's daughter dried his storm out of the sky. The victors locked him in the Gate of Alarm with the bowl still pouring, and in five thousand years nobody asked him to stop."],
	"pixiu": ["As a young beast of heaven Pixiu disgraced itself in the Hall of Audience, and the Court, which had no sense of humour, sealed it so that nothing it swallowed could come out. Then the Court noticed what that made it: a treasury that walked.", "For ten thousand years it was fed the tribute of the realms and never allowed to give anything away, always full and never satisfied. When the feeding stopped it began on the gilded roofs of the Gate, because hunger was all it had ever been given to keep."],
	"shentu": ["Shentu and his brother first kept the ghost-gate under the great peach tree of Dushuo, binding the wicked spirits with reeds for the tiger, and the Court liked the arrangement so much that it moved them, tree and tiger and all, to the Gate of Heaven.", "Shentu was the brother who opened. In ten thousand years he let through everyone who could look him in the eye, and fed the tiger peaches instead of spirits when he could, until it hardly knew what a ghost tasted like. He never stopped worrying that he was too soft."],
	"yulei": ["Yulei had not always been the brother who shut. Long ago, under the peach tree, he once let through a weeping spirit that Shentu had doubted, and it was a plague-ghost, and three villages were empty by morning.", "After that he did the shutting for both of them, so that his brother could go on being kind. When the Court told them to keep the Gate of Heaven shut, he did it more thoroughly than anyone had asked, and for four thousand years nobody got past his end of the rope."],
	"chong": ["Chong and Li were the grandsons of the sage-king Zhuanxu, and as boys they raced each other up and down the Jianmu, Chong always to the top, Li always to the roots. When their grandfather decided to cut the ladder, he sent each of them the way he liked best.", "Chong put his hands to heaven and pushed it up out of reach, and the last thing he heard, before the weight pressed his ears shut, was his little brother shouting something up the trunk from far below. In four thousand years he had never stopped wondering what it was."],
	"xingtian": ["Xingtian made music for the Flame Emperor once: songs for sowing, songs for harvest, and a dance the farmers did in the stubble when the millet was in. When his lord's house fell to the Yellow Emperor, he put down his flute and took up an axe.", "The Emperor cut off his head and buried it under Changyang Mountain, and Xingtian found eyes in his chest and a mouth in his belly and fought on. At the Gate of Opening he had danced for five thousand years; it was a harvest dance once, but he had forgotten the tune."],
	"root_wyrm": ["The wyrm hatched in the dark under the land the year the ladder was cut, the only egg of its clutch, and grew for four thousand years in a cold so complete that it believed the dark was the whole world, and the whole world was its own.", "Then a root as wide as a river came down through its sky, warm and humming with sap. The wyrm had never touched anything warm before and did not know what the feeling was; it decided the feeling was anger, and began to bite."],
	"pearl_mother": ["The traders above used to make the Pearl-Weepers cry on purpose, for the pearls, and it was the Pearl-Mother who went up to them in the children's place and wept until the traders were rich enough to go away. In a way, she never stopped.", "She drifted on the black water ever after, crying for anyone who could not, so that no child of Tearwater would ever have to weep for money again. When the springs turned bitter and the children's tears came out as grit, she wept harder, for all of them at once."],
	"wangxiang": ["The old books name the goblins of the springs in one breath, and they hatched together in one spring: Liang, who loved still water and sleep, and Wangxiang, who could not leave a pool alone without putting a claw in it to see what would happen.", "Five thousand years ago the victors of Zhuolu took Liang away to sing at the Gate of Rest, and Wangxiang was left with a spring that was suddenly much too clear. He had muddied every pool he found since, because a muddy pool might be hiding anything, even a brother."],
	"zhulong": ["For four thousand years Zhulong was the only day the Nine Springs had known: when he opened his eyes the black water shone red, when he shut them it was night, and every hour of the Pearl-Weepers was kept by his eyelids.", "Then the Jianmu's roots came down through the cavern roof glowing blue-green, and for the first time there was a light in his world that was not his. He shut his eyes to see whether anyone would miss him, and had lain in the dark for a month since, too proud to look."],
	"liang": ["Liang was a gentle goblin who loved still water, and the victors of Zhuolu chose him for exactly that. They set him in the black pool of the Gate of Rest with one task: to sing the war god in the grave below to sleep, and to drown anyone who came to wake him.", "He sang for five thousand years, the softest lullaby in the Nine Springs, and Chiyou never once closed his eyes. Liang did not know how to stop trying; he sang to all who came instead and held them under, kindly, and wondered if his brother still muddied springs."],
	"bai_wuchang": ["In life he was Xie Bi'an, tall and cheerful, and his sworn brother was a short, solemn man named Fan. One evening they agreed to meet under a bridge; it came on to rain, and Xie went home for an umbrella, telling Fan to wait.", "When he came back the river had risen over the bridge, and Fan had drowned sooner than break his word. Xie hanged himself from the same rail, hence his long tongue. Below, they were made Impermanence together, and he had been loudly cheerful since, enough for two."],
	"hei_wuchang": ["Fan Wujiu had never once broken his word in life. He promised his sworn brother to wait under the bridge, and when the rain came and the river rose, he held on to the bridge-post and went on waiting until the water closed over his hat.", "The black of his robe was the river's colour. He had been the second half of Impermanence ever since, collecting whatever his partner left over, as faithful in death as in life; and the words on his hat were the last thing he had wished for under that bridge."],
	"tubo": ["Houtu shaped Tubo first of all things out of the dark earth, giving him three eyes so that one would always be awake and nine coils so that nothing could get round him, and he had loved her ever since with the whole of his terrible heart.", "When the ladder was cut, the Court's order to keep the dead below and the living above came to him and not to her, because she never gave orders to anyone. He had kept it four thousand years, gnawing his thumbs bloody at the gate, waiting for her to want something."],
	"li": ["Li, the younger grandson, always raced his brother down the Jianmu to the roots while Chong raced to the top. The day the ladder was cut, he shouted up the trunk that he would be back in time for supper, and went down to press the earth flat.", "He pressed for four thousand years with an iron weight, on the very earth that was his mother, and never let himself think of supper, or of the brother he could not see at the other end of the world. Between them, he told himself, there had to be nothing at all."],
	"bronze_brothers": ["Chiyou had eighty-one brothers, bronze-headed and iron-browed like him, who worked the forges of the Nine Li at his side and ate sand and stone when the harvests failed. On the last day at Zhuolu he told the last of them to hold the line until he came back.", "They held it. The victors could not break them, so they built the Gate of Death round them where they stood, and for five thousand years the brothers stood shoulder to shoulder in the fog, chewing gravel, waiting for a brother nobody ever told them was dead."],
	"qianliyan": ["He and his brother were demons of Peach Blossom Mountain who both asked to marry the fisher-girl Lin Moniang. She would wed whichever could beat her; neither could, and with a red lantern and a great deal of patience she made them her generals instead.", "He watched the sea for her boats for a thousand years, proudly. When the tide went out and there were no boats, he thought she had no use for him and let a stranger pay him in pearls; and the one way he never looked was west, in case she had not noticed he was gone."],
	"shunfenger": ["The second of the two demon brothers Mazu beat and kept on as generals was the one with ears, who for a thousand years listened to every wind on the sea and told her which storms were coming for her boats.", "Of all the sounds in the world he loved her voice best. When the sea went out and she began to call the names of her lost boats every night, he could not bear to hear it, so he turned his ears east, to a stranger's wages and the far-off trees of a sunken isle."],
	"sea_monk": ["The fishermen of Landfall said the Sea-Monk had been a young novice crossing to an island temple, who laughed at the old boatmen for praying to Mazu, and was the only one aboard she did not bring home when the boat went down.", "The sea kept him, gave him a shell, and left him his shaved head and his laugh. He had resented fishermen for centuries, every one of them saved by a goddess he had scorned, and with the sea gone he no longer had to swim after them to make them sorry."],
	"longbo_giant": ["The old giants of Longbo, in their shrunken kingdom, told the same story every New Year: how great-great-grandfather walked out to the isles in a few strides and caught six turtles on one line, and how the Emperor, in his anger, made all of Longbo small.", "He grew up on that story, the smallest giant in a small land, with the six shells in his grandmother's house for telling fortunes. When the shrinking wore off, he walked east with the old hook to beat the record, and never thought to ask who lived on the isles."],
	"longevity_deer": ["The White Deer once carried the Old Man of the South Pole, the god of long life, from birthday to birthday all over the world, and was fed a herb of Fangzhang for every year it won for somebody's grandfather.", "When the Court hid the isles in its mirage, the old god was away on the mainland, and the deer was shut in without him. It waited two thousand years; when the garden began to die it panicked and ate, because it did not want to be old when he came back for it."],
	"elixir_furnace": ["The ninth furnace was the eldest of the House of Nine Furnaces, lit when the isle was young, and its one task was the elixir: water from the Returning Ruins, simmered for a thousand days. It had never once let its fire go out.", "When the water stopped, the other eight went cold one by one, and Furnace-Boy Dan polished them like gravestones. The ninth could not bear to go cold beside them, so it got up on its three legs and walked off, making anything at all, so long as it was still making."],
	"pearl_tree": ["Xu Fu stole his green fruit from this tree, the immortals said, and the tree felt the moment he bit it: a man stopping, like a spinning top pinned under a thumb. Later, through the same bitten fruit, a hungry boy of ten stopped too.", "The tree had not borne a single fruit since. It stood at the bottom of the garden drinking from the Ruins and refusing, year after year, to make anything that would stop anyone again; and when the water stopped coming too, it took that as one more reason."],
	"tianwu": ["Before the Court, Tianwu was the Water Earl of the valley of Zhaoyang, between two rivers, where each of his eight heads looked after one stream and they all got on well, because no stream was any other head's business.", "Then the Court gave him the stopper of Guixu: one task, one order, eight heads. They had argued for two thousand years about whose job it was, and when a stranger with a green fruit came saying the Court wanted it pulled, each head assumed another had checked."],
	"wu_zixu": ["Wu Zixu served the King of Wu for thirty years and warned him again and again that the King of Yue would one day come through the eastern gate. The king grew tired of hearing it, and sent him a sword with which to be quiet for good.", "Dying, he asked for his eyes to be hung over that gate to watch Yue march in; the king sewed him in a sack and threw him in the river. Yue came, as he had said. Being right never comforted him, and he had come up the river twice a day ever since to say it again."],
	"shen": ["In the days when nobody owned the sea, Shen breathed for pleasure: towers over the water at dawn, a market in the clouds at dusk, small gifts for fishermen who knew perfectly well they were not real and loved them anyway.", "The Court made her breathe a lie instead, a mirage to hide the isles, and for two thousand years every breath she drew kept the Thousand Children from anyone who might have come for them. When the sea went, she could not bear the empty bay, and breathed it full again."],
	"great_ao": ["The Great Ao had been sleeping out its sixty thousand years of rest in the deep when the giant of Longbo hooked six of its kin, two isles sank, and Yu Qiang blew the conch to call every turtle left back under the isles at once.", "It had held Yingzhou on its head for forty thousand years since without a shift, too tired to remember the sleep it was woken from. When the sea drained and the isle's weight came down on it with no water to bear it up, it began to thrash, and could not stop."],
	"yu_qiang": ["Yu Qiang blew the conch of the shifts once every sixty thousand years, and the turtles loved him for it. When the giant of Longbo took six and the shifts no longer added up, he asked the Emperor what to do, and was told to wait for orders.", "He waited forty thousand years. The turtles groaned under the isles all that time, and he let the green snakes coil in his ears so that he would not have to hear them; because if he heard them he would have to decide, and nobody had ever told him he could."],
	"red_banner": ["It was a banner once, a length of red hemp the women of the Nine Li dyed with madder and carried before their war god at Zhuolu, so that every warrior lost in the fog could see where home was. It fell in the last hour, and burned.", "Every autumn since, a red vapour rose from the grave like smoke from its ashes, and those who saw it called it Chiyou's Banner and were afraid. It had known nothing but the one war, and it showed that war to everyone who looked, hoping one of them would turn back."],
	"drowned_admiral": ["The Admiral had sworn to the First Emperor that the fleet would not come home without the elixir, and among the three thousand children his ships carried east was his own youngest daughter, chosen for the honour against his wishes.", "He saw her rowed ashore at Penglai and never saw her again; the sea took his warship and laid it on the slope of the Ruins. He had held course there in the dark for two thousand years, because turning back would mean he had failed her and the Emperor both."],
	"guixu_stopper": ["The Court cast the stopper to fit the throat of Guixu exactly and engraved on it every river in the world, so that each returning drop would pass under a seal and be signed for. It sat in the dark under the whole weight of the sea for two thousand years.", "It never minded the dark or the weight; it was made for them. Then, on a stranger's word, its keeper let it go, and it rolled out into the air and the light, which nothing had made it for, and it had rolled ever since, looking for a hole that would have it back."],
	"hai_ruo": ["When the River Earl came down to her in an autumn flood, swollen with pride, Ruo of the Northern Sea showed him her waters and told him, kindly, that even the sea was only a little thing between heaven and earth, a grain of rice in a granary.", "She had said it the way the great say such things, never expecting to find out it was true. Then a little man pulled the floor out from under her, and she felt herself pouring away into the dark, smaller by the hour, and she rose out of the drain in a fury of fear."],
	"xu_fu": ["Xu Fu carved three thousand names on bamboo by lamplight his first night ashore, and promised the children he would be back with the elixir before the youngest lost a tooth. On the hill the immortals told him no fruit would ripen for a hundred years.", "He could not wait a hundred years; a two-year-old called Ah Lan was waiting. So he stole a green fruit and bit it, and stopped, as a stone stops at the foot of a hill. He had decided not to go home without the elixir, and could never again decide anything else."],
	"mast_goblin": ["An old pine on the hills above Langya had housed the goblin Mei for a thousand years, and when the First Emperor's shipwrights cut it down for a mainmast, the goblin would not leave it, and went to sea.", "It held the sail of the Penglai-Seeker through every storm, and when the last storm took the ship it held up the sailors who clung to it as they drowned. Nobody was paid, nobody thanked, nobody sent home; and when the formation gave it a gate, it shut it on everyone."],
	"fetter_maple": ["The Emperor's smiths had no metal that could hold Chiyou, so they took the ploughshares of the Nine Li, the first ploughs, which he had forged himself, and beat them into fetters for him. They held. When he was dead they were struck off and thrown into the gorge.", "There they grew into the first maples, red as the blood on them, and kept their leaves through five thousand autumns, afraid of what would be left if they let anything go. The oldest had forgotten entirely that it was once made to open the earth, not to hold."],
	"chiyou": ["Chiyou found copper in the hills of the Nine Li and made the first plough, and walked the first furrow himself while nine tribes argued round one fire about whose field it should be. He laughed, they said, like a bell cracking, and he was kind to animals.", "Then a dry year came, the Emperor's people held the river, and the children of the Nine Li began to starve. He melted a plough into the first axe and never made anything else again. He died at Zhuolu with his eyes open, still trying to get his people to the water."],
	# ---- the people met on the road who come to blows with Lin Feng (fate.gd): their story is their thread
	"qiu_ye": ["Qiu Ye was the second son of a house that had lost everything but its pride, and the most gifted outer disciple of Azure Cloud until a sweeper outpaced him. His father wrote that he need not come home without a Foundation.", "What he became after that night in the storeroom depended on what the sweeper did about it, and he has never forgiven either of them for that."],
	"young_fang": ["Iron Fang's son was ten when he stood over his father's body with a kitchen knife. He kept his father's iron gauntlet and, for a while, his father's road: the silk bandits of Qingshi and Black Eel's river-rats called him Young Fang.", "Everything he knew about his father came from the man who killed him, or from bandits who never told the truth about anything."],
	"xu_lan": ["Demon Lord Xu Mo kept his only daughter in silver chains as a vessel for the moon's blood, so that the Blood Moon would outlive him. She was nineteen when the sweeper broke the palace, and she had never once been outside it.", "Whether she became her father's heir or her own woman was decided in three places: the inner sanctum, the shore of the Mirror Lake, and the rolls of Youdu."],
	"liu_ying": ["Liu Ying was six when her family sold her to the Marsh Hag in a famine, for a sack of millet. The Hag taught her every poison in the marsh and nothing else, and she was fourteen and sorting venoms when the Hag fell.", "Poison and medicine are the same knowledge pointed in different directions. Which way hers pointed, in the end, depended on who took her out of that hut and where."],
	# ---- Part XIV
	# the grudges that stayed where their owners fell (grudges.gd)
	"grudge_iron_fang": ["Iron Fang went over the bridge like any bandit, and the Wheel took him. His grudge did not go with him. It stayed in the bamboo where he fell, sharpening itself on one thought for longer than it can count.", "The thought is a boy of twelve at the sect gate, holding out his hands to be looked at, and a gate disciple who looked at the dirt under his nails instead of the qi in them. It has grown teeth since, and Nie has been drinking from it for years."],
	"grudge_marsh_hag": ["The Marsh Hag drowned in her own red water, and the Wheel took her soul, which was tired. It did not take her grudge, which was not. That stayed in the marsh among the lotuses she never stopped tending, and it keeps a ledger.", "Thirty-six families the Court sent into the marsh; thirty-six bills for cures she never sent, because there was nobody left to send them to. Every year the ledger grows longer and the name at the top of it fainter. She cannot read it any more. That is the worst of the debts."],
	"grudge_headless_saint": ["The Headless Sword Saint taught the Azure Cloud Sect its first bow, died refusing an Emperor, and refused him again when he was raised; Lin Feng laid him down among the graves. His body rests. His grudge never had a head to rest in.", "It is a small grudge and a very polite one: the sect he taught let his name fall off its ancestral tablets, because a saint who defied an Emperor was an awkward ancestor to have on the wall. Every year, at the hour the tablets are dusted, it bows to an empty space."],
	"grudge_fangfeng": ["Fangfeng was beheaded at Kuaiji for coming late to Yu's assembly, and Lin Feng put him down again when the Nine Cauldrons stirred. His bones fill a grave the size of a cart. His grudge stands in the breach of a dyke that is not there any more, holding back water that drained away four thousand years ago.", "It is a giant's grudge, and it has never once wanted revenge. It only wants to tell somebody about the village. It shouts at every traveller it sees, and none of them has ever stopped running long enough to listen."],
	"grudge_kui": ["Kui was born to hop in the surf of Liubo and bellow for the joy of it; the Yellow Emperor's hunters made a war drum of its hide, and it lived on in the clouds, bellowing first at every thunderclap so that nobody could beat it. Lin Feng stilled the drum. The grudge of the hide was not in the drum.", "It is in the sky over Juntian, a thunderhead the shape of a one-legged ox, and it has not bellowed for joy once in five thousand years. Every bolt it throws is the same bolt: the first beat of the drum at Zhuolu, the one that sent the Yellow Emperor's armies charging."],
	"grudge_admiral": ["The Admiral of the Lost Fleet held his course on the slope of the Returning Ruins for two thousand years until Lin Feng ended his watch, and his soul went over the bridge at last. His grudge stayed at the wheel of the wrecked flagship, where it had always been, waiting for word.", "It is the smallest grudge of the six and the heaviest: not against the First Emperor, or the sea, or Xu Fu who led them all east, but against himself, for letting them row his youngest daughter ashore at Penglai and never once going after her."],
	# ---- Part XV (the Lotus release): what came for Yue Ling
	"forgetting_queen": ["The Moth-Queen of Forgetting was once the empress of the silk moths of the mulberry woods, whom Lin Feng laid low long ago. What was left of her was very hungry and very patient, and when the Warden of the Lock went looking for something to eat a girl's past, it found her.", "She has a face like a blank page because she has eaten her own. She believes, quite sincerely, that she is being kind: nobody can hurt you by forgetting you if there is nothing left to forget."],
	"unweaver": ["The Unweaver is the Loom of Borders turned inside out: the hand that once wove every thread of every road, put to work by the Warden of the Lock winding the threads back onto the spindle. It is not cruel. It was made to finish things, and nobody ever told it which way.", "It has a hundred thin hands and no eyes, and it hums the weaving songs backwards. Every thread on its spindle is pink, and fine, and hers."],
	"lock_warden": ["When the Lotus Seal became a door at the Origin of the Dao, the part of it that had always been a lock was left with nothing to keep shut. For three hundred years it had taken Yue Ling back every time a bearer died and made her new. It called that keeping her safe.", "It calls itself Suo, the Warden, and it is not evil. It is frightened, the way locks are frightened of open doors: it believes that whatever is not kept will be lost. It means to keep her for ever, and it does not understand that a kept thing is not a living one."],
	# Part XVI (the Living Land release): the Last Night and the Year Beast
	# Part XVIII (the New Fire release)
	"zhurong": ["Zhurong is the Lord of the Southern Fire, set over summer when the world was made: a red giant with a beast's strength and a man's face, who rides two dragons of flame. In the old books he is the Fire Regulator. His whole office is to see that fire comes at the right time and goes when it should: lamps at dusk, stubble burnt after harvest, and at the turn of each season a new fire in every house.",
		"He keeps the drill with which Suiren made the first fire, and every summer for ten thousand years he has given new fire from it to hearths put out to receive it. Nobody has put a hearth out for sixty years, and nobody has come to ask. This summer somebody has come, with a pot of six old fires that all refused to die, and Zhurong, who has waited sixty years to be asked, is furious to be asked wrongly."],
	"huilu": ["Huilu is the name the old histories give the fire that comes to a house uninvited. When a city burned, the chroniclers wrote that Huilu had visited, the way one writes of a guest, because they knew what he was: not an enemy, but every fire that somebody lit and then walked away from, gone looking for the people who should have stayed.",
		"This one was born at Cloudfoot on the first evening of summer, when six keepers each lit a bonfire from the new fire to show the other five the proper way, and then turned their backs on the flames to quarrel. It has six colours in it that will not mix. It is not angry. It was lit and nobody sat up with it, and it is going from door to door to find out why."],
	# Part XVII (the Spring Thunder release)
	"qing_nu": ["Qing Nu is the Maiden of Frost, who comes down every autumn with her white hair loose and combs the frost out of it over the land, a night at a time, until spring sends her home. She is not cruel. The poets say she is the loneliest of the immortals, because nothing she touches stays awake to keep her company.",
		"This year she looked at the land under her snow, the graves and the burnt fields and the empty cradles of the longest year the world ever had, and decided that it was kinder to let it all go on sleeping. She has been combing for a month, and the frost is getting thicker."],
	"taisui": ["Taisui is the lord of the year under the earth: a great soft lump of living flesh with eyes all over it, who moves through the ground every year opposite the year star in the sky. Wherever he lies, the ground is his head; whoever breaks it, for a well or a grave or a furrow, has broken it on Taisui, and the year goes badly for that house.",
		"People who know this light incense to him at the start of every year and ask his pardon before they dig. This year nobody did, and this spring the whole land was broken open at once: the ox, the furrows, the rockfalls, the ice, the deadfall. Every blow of it landed on him, and he has not slept since."],
	"poison_mother": ["The five poisons, scorpion, centipede, snake, toad and spider, wake with the first thunder of spring and are at their worst on the fifth day of the fifth month, when people hang mugwort on the doors and paint the children's foreheads with realgar. Every one of them has a mother: a toad as big as a granary with a scorpion's tail and a centipede's hundred legs, who sleeps under the driest ground in the land.",
		"This year the driest ground in the land was the Scorched Millet Plains, cracked open by nine days of noon, and she made her nest in the cracks and kept her children warm in them all winter. When the thunder came she woke with all of them at once, and came up to see who had done it."],
	"lean_king": ["Every hard year has a last month when the jars are empty and the new rice is not in, and every hard last month has a king: a crowned skeleton on a throne of empty jars, counting the grain that is left from one hand into the other.", "He is not cruel. He only counts, and every year the count comes to nothing, because he never thinks to count the handful every family hides at the bottom of the jar for New Year's Eve. Nian eats what the Lean Month King leaves; this year the King counted for the whole frightened land, and left Nian a feast."],
	"xi_beast": ["Before there were years there were nights, and the last night of every year was a long dark coil called Xi that ate whatever light was left burning. People learned to leave nothing out for it: they put out every lamp but one, and sat round that one together, and Xi found nothing worth eating in a house where the light was shared.", "Then one year a boy set off bamboo in a fire in its face, and Xi fled into the mountains, and the last night of the year has been called Chuxi, the Night Xi Was Driven Off, ever since. Xi has never forgiven the name. It has only been waiting for a year when everybody was too tired to light a fire."],
	"nian": ["Nian sleeps under the mountains for all of the year but one night, and while it sleeps it eats: every fear anybody in the land had that year goes down into the dark where it lies, and it grows on them. On New Year's Eve it wakes and comes down to the villages to eat the rest: the harvest, the cattle, and anybody left alone in the dark.", "Once an old beggar came to a village on New Year's Eve and stayed behind when everybody else fled. He pasted red paper on the doors, lit a fire and threw bamboo in it, and Nian ran from the noise and the red and the light. The beggar was never seen again. The red, the fire and the noise stayed, and so did Nian, waiting for a year when everybody forgot."],
	"nie": ["Nie is as old as the first unpaid debt. It is not a demon or a god but a reckoning: the soot every grudge leaves at the Wheel of the Six Paths, every promise half-kept, every thread cut instead of tied, standing up at last with many hands.", "It grew all the way up the mountain beside Lin Feng, fed by three thousand foes he put down and never asked about, and it has come to collect."],
}

## A foe's story: one paragraph ("" if it has none).
static func story(id: String) -> String:
	if BOSS.has(id):
		return " ".join(BOSS[id])
	return str(BEAST.get(id, ""))
